window.ADAPTIVE_EXAM_PAPERS=[{"id":"exam-11","examNumber":11,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 01","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"11 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 01","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Messages from the Stone Age","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["The incredible pre-historic Chauvet cave art in France is painted in 0 _____ colours and dates back to a period around thirty thousand years ago when early humans first started to create rock art. Although various ",{"n":1,"options":["illustrations","models","cases","examples"],"answer":"examples","answers":["examples"],"explanation":"D - examples. 'Illustrations' of art would mean visual copies of it, e.g. a photo of that art. 'Models' and 'cases' do not collocate with 'art'.","skill":"Part 1"}," of this art have been found in caves in Western Europe, very few people have seen the art at Chauvet because it is located ",{"n":2,"options":["deep","thick","long","dense"],"answer":"deep","answers":["deep"],"explanation":"A - deep. 'Deep inside' is the only appropriate collocation - the other three words do not make any working word combinations. 'Thick inside' is unnatural, 'log inside' is illogical and 'dense inside' works for context like crowds or forests.","skill":"Part 1"}," inside an inaccessible underground cave system. Those who have seen it say that it is very impressive, showing animals ",{"n":3,"options":["by means of","apart from","as well as","such as"],"answer":"such as","answers":["such as"],"explanation":"D - such as. This expression is the only one that shows examples or introduces a list. 'By means of' means 'by or using something'. 'Apart from' introduces an exception, e.g.: 'Everybody apart from Tom attended the conference'. 'As well as' adds something to the list, e.g.: 'The shops sold soft drinks, as well as cigarettes and frozen pizzas'.","skill":"Part 1"}," horses, rhinos and cows, and that the artwork is good enough to ",{"n":4,"options":["rival","compare","compete","oppose"],"answer":"rival","answers":["rival"],"explanation":"A - rival. 'Compare' and 'compete' require 'with' in this context (e.g. 'compete with something'). Moreover, 'rival' means to be as good as something or someone else, and to compete with is to try to win. 'Oppose' means to be against something (negative meaning).","skill":"Part 1"}," modern compositions. The first scientists to ",{"n":5,"options":["arrive","reach","meet","know"],"answer":"reach","answers":["reach"],"explanation":"B - reach. This is the only word with the meaning 'to arrive at a place or destination'.","skill":"Part 1"}," the Chauvet paintings missed some other important ",{"n":6,"options":["instances","matters","details","issues"],"answer":"details","answers":["details"],"explanation":"C - details. 'Details' is the only word that collocates with the verb 'to miss'. 'Details' are usually minor and easy to miss, while 'matters' are more significant. 'Issues' means 'problems or difficulties'. 'Miss matters' and 'miss issues' are possible in other contexts but don't fit here because the reference is to overlooked features of the cave walls.","skill":"Part 1"}," however. The walls of the cave are also marked with a series of lines and symbols, that were initially ",{"n":7,"options":["believed","regarded","thought","agreed"],"answer":"regarded","answers":["regarded"],"explanation":"B - regarded. The only option that can be followed by 'as'. 'Believed' and 'thought' are normally followed by 'to be': 'She is believed to be the best specialist in the field'.","skill":"Part 1"}," as insignificant. But recent research has suggested that these marks may represent humankind's first steps towards the development of writing, which is ",{"n":8,"options":["resulting","having","making","causing"],"answer":"causing","answers":["causing"],"explanation":"D - causing. 'Resulting' would need 'in' to work. 'Having' doesn't fit grammatically here. Finally, 'making' is fine grammatically, but it is more forceful, implying direct pressure that is put on people. 'Causing' is more neutral and fitting the general academic style of the paragraph.","skill":"Part 1"}," people to rethink their ideas about when written communication first started."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Swimming with seals","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["The UK is home to half the world's population of grey seals and there 0 _____ many local boat trips offering sightseeing tours out to the islands and sandbanks ",{"n":9,"answer":"where","answers":["where"],"explanation":"where. A relative pronoun is needed here. Seals can often be found resting on (or near) sandbanks.","skill":"Part 2"}," the animals are most regularly found. But if you really want to get close ",{"n":10,"answer":"to","answers":["to"],"explanation":"to. 'Close' is normally used with 'to' if we talk about physical distance, e.g. if we are so close that we can touch them. 'Close with' would mean connection such as friendship, which is not mentioned in this context.","skill":"Part 2"}," the seals and understand something about their way of life, then you need to go on an underwater seal-watching trip. On these trips, you have the chance to go over the side of the boat and, equipped ",{"n":11,"answer":"with","answers":["with"],"explanation":"with. 'Equipped with' is used when talking about articles of clothing or necessary things, in this case - a mask, a snorkel and so on.","skill":"Part 2"}," a wetsuit, mask and snorkel, spend time in the water alongside the animals. Seals are extremely inquisitive creatures and, once you're in the water, they will swim past you trying to work ",{"n":12,"answer":"out","answers":["out"],"explanation":"out. 'To work out' is a synonym of 'to find out' - to learn the meaning or the reason of something, to discover or understand something.","skill":"Part 2"}," who you are and ",{"n":13,"answer":"what","answers":["what"],"explanation":"what. A conjunction connecting two clauses. A clause is normally a subject and a verb in case you forgot.","skill":"Part 2"}," you're doing there. ",{"n":14,"answer":"Although","answers":["Although","Though","While"],"explanation":"Although/Though/While. A linking word used to contrast two parts of the following sentence. Remember that in your answer sheet all of the letters have to be capital, the capitalisation here is to indicate that it's the first word of the sentence.","skill":"Part 2"}," they can appear shy at first, seals soon ",{"n":15,"answer":"get","answers":["get"],"explanation":"get. 'To get used to' means 'to become comfortable with something new'.","skill":"Part 2"}," used to you being in the water, and will come and play around you. Young pups especially like to ",{"n":16,"answer":"make","answers":["make","have"],"explanation":"make/have. Both words collocate with 'contact'.","skill":"Part 2"}," contact with  divers, and often use their teeth, gently biting masks, fins and cameras out of curiosity. It can be a thrilling experience."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Brain games","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["According to experts, doing puzzles keeps our brains fit and 0 _____. As well as gaining ",{"n":17,"answer":"satisfaction","answers":["satisfaction"],"explanation":"satisfaction. Verb to noun transformation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SATISFY"}," from finding the correct answer to a difficult problem, we give our brains a good workout in the process. To help us do this, all sorts of handheld 'brain games' are now available in the shops, and the most ",{"n":18,"answer":"successful","answers":["successful"],"explanation":"successful. Mind the spelling! Double 'c', double 's', one 'l'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SUCCESS"}," games have sold in their millions. What's more, people ",{"n":19,"answer":"discover","answers":["discover"],"explanation":"discover. The correct transformation to a verb that means 'to find out, to realise'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"COVER"}," that the more they play the games, the easier it is to find a ",{"n":20,"answer":"solution","answers":["solution"],"explanation":"solution. Verb to noun transformation. It might not be evident that 'solve' and 'solution' are morphologically related because of all the changes you have to make during transformation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SOLVE"}," to the problems posed. They see this as proof that there has been an ",{"n":21,"answer":"improvement","answers":["improvement"],"explanation":"improvement. Verb becomes a noun. Pay attention to the spelling.","skill":"Part 3","base":"IMPROVE"}," in the power of their brains. Unfortunately, however, this may be a false impression. Some ",{"n":22,"answer":"scientists","answers":["scientists"],"explanation":"scientists. Make sure to pluralise the noun, as suggested by 'some' at the beginning of the sentence and the following 'argue'. Another difficult word to spell.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SCIENCE"}," argue that the brain gets better at any task the more often it is repeated. In other words, the improvement in the ",{"n":23,"answer":"performance","answers":["performance"],"explanation":"performance. Verb to noun transformation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"PERFORM"}," of the brain is something that happens naturally. So although these brain games are obviously fun to play, it remains ",{"n":24,"answer":"uncertain","answers":["uncertain"],"explanation":"uncertain. A prefix is used to make the adjective negative - as suggested by the contrasting 'although' at the beginning of the sentence.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CERTAIN"}," whether they are actually helping to boost brainpower or not."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Brad speaks English better than his parents do.","keyword":"AS","secondBefore":"Brad's parents don't ","secondAfter":" he does.","answers":["speak English as well as"],"display":"speak English as well as","explanation":"speak English as well as. A comparison 'as well as' is hinted at by 'as' in the task.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"Cycling is not allowed in the park.","keyword":"SUPPOSED","secondBefore":"You ","secondAfter":" in the park.","answers":["are not supposed to cycle"],"display":"are not supposed to cycle","explanation":"are not supposed to cycle. Passive form of the verb is required.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"'I'm sorry I didn't let you know I was going to be late, Ann,' said Jamie.","keyword":"APOLOGISED","secondBefore":"Jamie ","secondAfter":" Ann know that he was going to be late.","answers":["apologised for not letting"],"display":"apologised for not letting","explanation":"apologised for not letting. Reporting verb with gerund is used.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"A heavy fall of snow prevented them from getting home that night.","keyword":"ABLE","secondBefore":"They ","secondAfter":" home that night because of a heavy fall of snow.","answers":["were not able to go","were not able to get"],"display":"were not able to go","explanation":"were not able to go/get. Remember that you can't change the given word, so 'were unable to go/get' can't be used, even though it is grammatically and contextually correct.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"Ursula was disappointed not to win the competition.","keyword":"CAME","secondBefore":"Not winning the competition ","secondAfter":" to Ursula.","answers":["came as a disappointment"],"display":"came as a disappointment","explanation":"came as a disappointment. 'Come as a disappointment' is a fixed expression. 'Came' matches the past tense of 'was dissapointed', while 'come as' as a structure effectively expresses the speaker's emotional reaction. Be attentive to spell 'disappointment' correctly.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"I paid far more money for my new computer than I expected to.","keyword":"SO","secondBefore":"I didn't expect ","secondAfter":" money for my new computer.","answers":["to pay so much"],"display":"to pay so much","explanation":"to pay so much. A comparative expression with 'so'.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-12","examNumber":12,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 02","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"12 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 02","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Where to go whale watching","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["When asked to list the things they would most like to experience in life, a surprising 0 _____ of people mention seeing whales in their natural habitat. It's an ambition that can be ",{"n":1,"options":["answered","rewarded","honoured","fulfilled"],"answer":"fulfilled","answers":["fulfilled"],"explanation":"D - fulfilled. 'To fulfil an ambition' is the only correct collocation here. However, we can say 'to achieve goals', but obviously in this task we can't change the text given.","skill":"Part 1"}," surprisingly easily. It is ",{"n":2,"options":["estimated","counted","guessed","totalled"],"answer":"estimated","answers":["estimated"],"explanation":"A - estimated. Only 'estimated' and 'counted' can be used in passive form correctly. 'Estimated' is used when giving an approximate number. 'Counted' would imply an exact figure, which is unlikely with five thousand whales. 'Guess' points at lack of evidence and is too informal for this context.","skill":"Part 1"}," that the seas around Iceland are home to over five thousand orca whales. But their behaviour, and therefore your chances of seeing them, varies ",{"n":3,"options":["according","depending","relying","agreeing"],"answer":"according","answers":["according"],"explanation":"A - according. The only word that can be used with the preposition 'to'. 'Depending' and 'relying' both need the preposition 'on', 'agreeing' takes 'with'.","skill":"Part 1"}," to the season. In summer, the whales have a ",{"n":4,"options":["custom","tendency","habit","trend"],"answer":"tendency","answers":["tendency"],"explanation":"B - tendency. Answers A and D do not fit contextually as they have different meanings. 'Habit' can't be used because it should be followed by a gerund: 'be in the habit of doing something'.","skill":"Part 1"}," to hang out near the coast and can be seen swimming up fjords and inlets. During the winter months, however, the animals are generally to be found ",{"n":5,"options":["longer","wider","broader","further"],"answer":"further","answers":["further"],"explanation":"D - further. 'Further out' is the only correct collocation of the four - it means at a greater distance from the shore.","skill":"Part 1"}," out at sea. ",{"n":6,"options":["Whenever","Whoever","Whichever","However"],"answer":"Whichever","answers":["Whichever"],"explanation":"C - Whichever. The only determiner that can be used with a noun here - we are referring to seasons, so 'whoever' doesn't work. 'However' is more about the way you do something. 'Whenever' would fit if the sentence went like 'Whenever you choose to go on your trip'.","skill":"Part 1"}," season you choose for your trip, whale-watching trips are very easy to organise, and there's a chance you'll get to see other whale species too. Besides Iceland, another option is to ",{"n":7,"options":["head","set","point","pick"],"answer":"head","answers":["head"],"explanation":"A - head. 'To head for' means 'to go in a certain direction'. 'To set off' is a phrasal verb with the same meaning, but there is no participle 'off' for it to work.","skill":"Part 1"}," for northern Norway between October and January. Orcas arrive here at this time of year in ",{"n":8,"options":["hunt","follow","pursuit","seek"],"answer":"pursuit","answers":["pursuit"],"explanation":"C - pursuit. 'In pursuit of something' means 'seeking, chasing something'. Other nouns do not fit 'in ... of something' construction.","skill":"Part 1"}," of large shoals of herring, which form an important part of their diet."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · The Mini-Monet","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["Kieron Williamson is a very successful landscape painter. His paintings, 0 _____ mostly depict the peaceful countryside around his home village in eastern England, sell very well. Perhaps this shouldn't ",{"n":9,"answer":"come","answers":["come"],"explanation":"come. 'Come as a surprise' is a set expression - note the indefinite article. If something comes as a surprise to you, then you do not expect it, you do not see it coming.","skill":"Part 2"}," as that much of a surprise. It's a beautiful part of the world, and landscape paintings are always popular.  The strange thing about Kieron, however, ",{"n":10,"answer":"is","answers":["is"],"explanation":"is. 'The strange thing ... is that'. We rearrange words in the sentence for dramatic effect or to stress a particular part of it.","skill":"Part 2"}," that he is only seven years old. At a recent exhibition of his work, all the paintings were sold in half an hour, some for £100,000 each. Kieron now has fans all over the world, and has gained the nickname the 'Mini-Monet'. What's even ",{"n":11,"answer":"more","answers":["more"],"explanation":"more. 'Even more' is used to make the meaning more intense. Another case of an emphatic structure.","skill":"Part 2"}," surprising is that Kieron doesn't come from an artistic family, and only started painting two years ",{"n":12,"answer":"ago","answers":["ago"],"explanation":"ago. A time in the past is mentioned. Be careful not to answer 'later' - as the word requires a reference point, e.g. 'he got interested in music and two years later he started making it'.","skill":"Part 2"},". He first got inspiration ",{"n":13,"answer":"from","answers":["from"],"explanation":"from. 'To get inspiration from something or someone' - this is when somebody inspires you to do something.","skill":"Part 2"}," some boats he saw on holiday at the seaside. Kieron asked his parents if they ",{"n":14,"answer":"would","answers":["would","could"],"explanation":"would/could. Both modals are acceptable here - with both meanings fitting the context.","skill":"Part 2"}," buy him a sketch pad and pencils ",{"n":15,"answer":"so","answers":["so"],"explanation":"so. 'So that' is the phrase that shows the purpose of doing, a shorter version of 'in order to be able to do something'.","skill":"Part 2"}," that he could draw them. Only when they saw ",{"n":16,"answer":"how","answers":["how"],"explanation":"how. They talk about the skill of Kieron's drawing. We can see an example of an inverted sentence here - here we use an auxiliary verb (did) in a positive sentence for dramatic effect.","skill":"Part 2"}," well he drew did they realise that he was talented."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Don't catch cold","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["According to recent 0 _____, at least some of the advice about health that was passed down to our grandparents by previous generations may actually contain some ",{"n":17,"answer":"truth","answers":["truth"],"explanation":"truth. A very straightforward adjective to noun transformation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"TRUE"},". A good example is the ",{"n":18,"answer":"relationship","answers":["relationship"],"explanation":"relationship. Do not pluralise the noun, otherwise it won't be counted as correct here - after all, there is only one relationship in the sentence - one of feeling cold and catching a cold.","skill":"Part 3","base":"RELATION"}," between feeling cold and catching a cold. My grandmother was convinced that being outside when the temperature is low, or simply dressing in ",{"n":19,"answer":"unsuitable","answers":["unsuitable"],"explanation":"unsuitable. Note the negative prefix suggested by the context of the sentence. We talk about the kind of clothing that would (or wouldn't) work in colder environments.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SUIT"}," clothes for the cold weather, was a sure way of catching a cold. Now, as we know, colds are caused by viruses. Therefore, in the ",{"n":20,"answer":"absence","answers":["absence"],"explanation":"absence. Adjective to noun transformation. Make sure to spell the word with 'c'. 'In the absence of something' means when something isn't there, especially if normally it should be present.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ABSENT"}," of a virus, you cannot catch a cold - no matter how low the temperature or how ",{"n":21,"answer":"inappropriate","answers":["inappropriate"],"explanation":"inappropriate. Similar case to Question 19, a negative prefix is needed. Spelling is a bit tricky, with the double 'p'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"APPROPRIATE"}," your clothing. But ",{"n":22,"answer":"scientists","answers":["scientists"],"explanation":"scientists. The plural forms is what we need here, as suggested by 'think' - the verb that the subject has to agree with.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SCIENCE"}," now think that we may have viruses in our bodies already, which are just waiting for the chance to turn into a cold. In cold weather, for example, blood vessels in the nose get smaller to stop heat escaping. ",{"n":23,"answer":"Unfortunately","answers":["Unfortunately"],"explanation":"Unfortunately. Attacks from viruses is something undesirable, so a negative prefix fits nicely.","skill":"Part 3","base":"FORTUNATE"},", also allows the cold virus to attack the nose or throat more ",{"n":24,"answer":"easily","answers":["easily"],"explanation":"easily. A comparative 'more' modifies the adverb 'easily' that we get from changing the adjective 'easy'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"EASY"},". So, perhaps Granny was right! "]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Tania hasn't played volleyball for three years.","keyword":"SINCE","secondBefore":"It ","secondAfter":" Tania last played volleyball.","answers":["has been three years since","has is three years since"],"display":"has been three years since","explanation":"has been/is three years since. Both variants are acceptable. 'Since' as the keyword means that a perfect tense would fit well here.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"The only thing Carl forgot to buy was a new battery for his watch.","keyword":"EVERYTHING","secondBefore":"Carl remembered to ","secondAfter":" a new battery for his watch.","answers":["buy everything except"],"display":"buy everything except","explanation":"buy everything except. Note how changing 'forgot' to 'remembered' alters the sentence - a positive to negative transformation takes place.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"John was angry because Claire arrived late.","keyword":"TIME","secondBefore":"If Claire ","secondAfter":" John would not have been angry.","answers":["had arrived on time"],"display":"had arrived on time","explanation":"had arrived on time. Past perfect is used to show how one thing happened after the other. A third conditional sentence here shows the unreal result of an unreal action.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"I don't usually wear a hat and so it feels strange.","keyword":"USED","secondBefore":"I am ","secondAfter":" a hat and so it feels strange.","answers":["not used to wearing"],"display":"not used to wearing","explanation":"not used to wearing. We need a gerund after 'used to'. If you are not used to doing something, it means it is new to you, you are not accustomed to it.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"Rob said to Jennifer: 'You broke the strap on my new camera.'","keyword":"ACCUSED","secondBefore":"Rob ","secondAfter":" the strap on his new camera.","answers":["accused Jennifer of breaking"],"display":"accused Jennifer of breaking","explanation":"accused Jennifer of breaking. Accuse somebody of something. Naturally, 'accuse' only works with negative actions, when you blame somebody for something.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"Mark can't wait to use his new computer-games console.","keyword":"FORWARD","secondBefore":"Mark is ","secondAfter":" his new computer games console.","answers":["(really) looking forward to using"],"display":"(really) looking forward to using","explanation":"(really) looking forward to using. Both variants can be used here. To look forward to something means that you are really excited about it happening in the future.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-13","examNumber":13,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 03","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"13 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 03","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · The Gesture Interface","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["At the 0 _____ if we want mobile ",{"n":1,"options":["line","entry","access","link"],"answer":"access","answers":["access"],"explanation":"C - access. The only acceptable collocation here is 'mobile access'. 'Mobile line' is a general term for communication through a cellular system. The remaining two options make no common collocations.","skill":"Part 1"}," to the Web, we have to travel around with things like tablets and smartphones in our bags or pockets. But the inventor of something called the Gesture Interface has ",{"n":2,"options":["come","thought","found","done"],"answer":"come","answers":["come"],"explanation":"A - come. 'To come up' is a phrasal verb that means to invent, to think of something useful. 'Think up' is a similar expression that means 'to invent something, to create something unique', but it cannot be used next to 'with'. 'To do up' means to repair something, to give something a quick fix.","skill":"Part 1"}," up with a way of doing this using only something we've ",{"n":3,"options":["delivered","fetched","brought","carried"],"answer":"carried","answers":["carried"],"explanation":"D - carried. 'To carry around' means to have something on you, ready to be used.","skill":"Part 1"}," around with us for millions of years - our hands. The idea is ",{"n":4,"options":["based","started","founded","begun"],"answer":"based","answers":["based"],"explanation":"A - based. If something (e.g. an idea) is based on something, it relies on it heavily in order to work. 'To found something' is to invent it, and it requires no preposition.","skill":"Part 1"}," on simple technology that is already in existence. ",{"n":5,"options":["Along with","Instead of","Except for","Apart from"],"answer":"Instead of","answers":["Instead of"],"explanation":"B - Instead of. A replacement to the 'usual hardware' is suggested. 'Along with' would mean in addition to the usual hardware - something that is not implied.","skill":"Part 1"}," carrying around the usual hardware, you have a small wearable device on your hands. This contains a projector, a camera and wireless technology to ",{"n":6,"options":["sign","download","log","connect"],"answer":"connect","answers":["connect"],"explanation":"D - connect. 'Sign' and 'log' require 'in' following it. 'Download' doesn't make sense in this context.","skill":"Part 1"}," you to the Web. It also has sensors which read and interpret simple hand gestures. For example, when you want to ",{"n":7,"options":["make","take","do","get"],"answer":"take","answers":["take"],"explanation":"B - take. 'Take a photograph, take a picture are the common collocations. A good example of how good knowledge of collocation could give you better chances of scoring high. You might feel like 'do a photograph' or 'make a photograph' work as well, but they don't.","skill":"Part 1"}," a photograph, simply make a frame around the image with your hand and click your fingers. The device ",{"n":8,"options":["works","reckons","meets","knows"],"answer":"works","answers":["works"],"explanation":"A - works. 'To work out' is another phrasal verb with the meaning 'to understand, to realise'.","skill":"Part 1"}," out what you want and operates the camera."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Choosing the best shades","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["Most of us buy a new pair 0 _____ fashionable sunglasses each year. To be honest, we probably ",{"n":9,"answer":"put","answers":["put"],"explanation":"put. 'To put thought into something' is to give something consideration, to think about something, or to be interested in it.","skill":"Part 2"}," more thought into looking cool than we do into ensuring that the glasses match our needs. It seems an obvious thing to say, ",{"n":10,"answer":"but","answers":["but"],"explanation":"but. The conjunction contrasts these two parts of the sentence. Note that 'however' cannot be used as we would need a comma after it.","skill":"Part 2"}," the main role of sunglasses isn't actually to enhance your image. It is rather to protect your eyes from the potentially harmful rays of the sun. What's ",{"n":11,"answer":"more","answers":["more"],"explanation":"more. 'What is more' is used to introduce additional information to something that has already been mentioned.","skill":"Part 2"},", price isn't necessarily the best guide ",{"n":12,"answer":"to","answers":["to"],"explanation":"to. A guide to something. 'A guide of' is something you might feel like choosing - don't, it is much less common, and chiefly used in American English.","skill":"Part 2"}," the effectiveness of sunglasses. Indeed, expensive designer glasses may not provide the best defence against sun damage. ",{"n":13,"answer":"although","answers":["although","though","while"],"explanation":"although/though/while. Any of the three conjunctions can be used with the meaning remaining unchanged - adding contrast to the sentence.","skill":"Part 2"}," you may still want to choose a pair of sunglasses that look good on you, it's also crucial to check out just how effective they will be in protecting your eyes.  There are three types of light ",{"n":14,"answer":"which","answers":["which"],"explanation":"which. 'Which' introduces a defining relative clause referring to 'types of light'. While 'that' is sometimes possible in defining clauses, 'which' is preferred in formal written English.","skill":"Part 2"}," can harm the human eye. They are known ",{"n":15,"answer":"as","answers":["as"],"explanation":"as. 'Known as' has the meaning of 'called, referred to'.","skill":"Part 2"}," ultra-violet light, visible light and infra-red light. If you ",{"n":16,"answer":"take","answers":["take"],"explanation":"take. 'To take something seriously' is to approach something with due consideration, to see something as important. Funnily enough, we could technically say 'to approach something seriously', but it is much more common to say 'to approach doing something seriously'.","skill":"Part 2"}," the choice of sunglasses seriously, then you will want to protect yourself against all three."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Rafting on the Zambezi river","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Victoria Falls on the Zambezi river in Africa is one of the most 0 _____ sights in the world. It is also an ",{"n":17,"answer":"incredible","answers":["incredible"],"explanation":"incredible. Adjective to negative adjective transformation using a prefix. Even though we don't have a lot of context to play with, we definitely need an adjective and the only other option is leaving the word as-is, which you should never do in FCE Use of English Part 3.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CREDIBLE"}," place for water sports. The translation of the local Zambian name for the falls is 'the smoke that thunders' and it's a pretty good ",{"n":18,"answer":"description","answers":["description"],"explanation":"description. Verb-to-noun transformation. Please note that 'b' from 'describe' changes to 'p' in 'description'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DESCRIBE"},". On arrival, it's the noise that makes the greatest ",{"n":19,"answer":"impression","answers":["impression"],"explanation":"impression. Make sure to spell the noun correctly.","skill":"Part 3","base":"IMPRESS"}," on you as the water drops 108 metres in a waterfall that is nearly two kilometres wide. The ",{"n":20,"answer":"surrounding","answers":["surrounding"],"explanation":"surrounding. Verb-to-adjective transformation. This is a rare transformation when you just slap '-ing' at the end and call it a day. Almost too easy! Do mind the spelling.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SURROUND"}," landscape is also awesome, and well worth a visit. You can't go white-water rafting over the falls themselves, but the rapids ",{"n":21,"answer":"farther","answers":["farther","further"],"explanation":"farther/further. Both comparative adjectives are acceptable. Be attentive to spelling. Since this refers to physical rapids down a river, farther is slightly more precise — but Cambridge accepts both.","skill":"Part 3","base":"FAR"}," down the Zambezi river provide a very exciting location. Although the rapids are given grades five and six on the sports ",{"n":22,"answer":"difficulty","answers":["difficulty"],"explanation":"difficulty. Do not pluralise the noun - the word 'sports' might confuse you here. A difficulty scale is an attempt to show how difficult something is - in this particular case, using numbers.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DIFFICULT"}," scale, there are places where relative ",{"n":23,"answer":"beginners","answers":["beginners"],"explanation":"beginners. This time we are looking for a plural noun, as suggested by the pronoun 'they' further in the sentence. Remember that subject-verb agreement is a common area where students fail.","skill":"Part 3","base":"BEGIN"}," can have a go, as long as they are accompanied by experienced operators. These people guide you through the rapids and ensure that you don't stray into the more ",{"n":24,"answer":"dangerous","answers":["dangerous"],"explanation":"dangerous. Noun-to-adjective transformation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DANGER"}," sections by mistake."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"The band probably won't do another tour this year.","keyword":"UNLIKELY","secondBefore":"The band ","secondAfter":" another tour this year.","answers":["is unlikely to do"],"display":"is unlikely to do","explanation":"is unlikely to do. 'Unlikely' expresses the idea of low probability. Do not confuse this with so-called double negative, which is rarely used in English.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"It's very hard for Alex to get up in the morning.","keyword":"TROUBLE","secondBefore":"Alex ","secondAfter":" up in the morning.","answers":["has (great) trouble getting"],"display":"has (great) trouble getting","explanation":"has (great) trouble getting. To have trouble doing something. 'Great' can be omitted.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"Lily is so good at swimming that she is going to train for the Olympics.","keyword":"SUCH","secondBefore":"Lily ","secondAfter":" that she is going to train for the Olympics.","answers":["is such a good swimmer"],"display":"is such a good swimmer","explanation":"is such a good swimmer. Don't forget to include the indefinite article 'a' as we are referring to a countable noun in the singular.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"One of us ought to go and meet the visitor at the airport.","keyword":"SHOULD","secondBefore":"The visitor ","secondAfter":" one of us at the airport.","answers":["should be met by"],"display":"should be met by","explanation":"should be met by. We transform active voice in the original to passive.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"I went to the dentist's because I couldn't stand the toothache any longer.","keyword":"UP","secondBefore":"I couldn't ","secondAfter":" the toothache any longer, so I went to the dentist's.","answers":["put up with"],"display":"put up with","explanation":"put up with. 'To put up with' is to endure something, to accept something unpleasant.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"I wasn't invited to the party and feel sad about that.","keyword":"WISH","secondBefore":"I ","secondAfter":" invited to the party.","answers":["wish I had been"],"display":"wish I had been","explanation":"wish I had been. Past perfect passive is used here to express regret about something that didn't happen.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-14","examNumber":14,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 04","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"14 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 04","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Royal Residences","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["Buckingham Palace, Windsor Castle 0 _____ the Palace of Holyroodhouse are these ",{"n":1,"options":["venues","residences","situations","occupation"],"answer":"residences","answers":["residences"],"explanation":"B - residences. A residence is a place where one 'resides', or lives. A venue is a place where something happens, i. e. a stadium is a good venue for a music festival. The other two words do not fit the context.","skill":"Part 1"}," of the Sovereign and, as such, serve as both home and office for the Queen, whose personal flag flies ",{"n":2,"options":["whatever","however","whoever","whenever"],"answer":"whenever","answers":["whenever"],"explanation":"D - whenever. The meaning here is that at any given time the Queen is there, the flags mark her presence.","skill":"Part 1"}," her Majesty is in residence. These buildings are used extensively for State ceremonies and official entertaining and are opened to the ",{"n":3,"options":["humans","public","peoples","strangers"],"answer":"public","answers":["public"],"explanation":"B - public. 'Open to the public' is a set phrase that means that it accepts visitors, just like an exhibition or a museum would.","skill":"Part 1"}," as much as these commitments allow. They are furnished with fine pictures and works of art from the Royal Collection, assembled over four centuries by successive sovereigns. Many of the State Apartments and rooms at the official residences have been in continuous use since their conception and many of the paintings are ",{"n":4,"options":["created","explored","produced","displayed"],"answer":"displayed","answers":["displayed"],"explanation":"D - displayed. This verb is the most commonly used when talking about showing various exhibits, such as paintings, sculptures or other forms of visual art.","skill":"Part 1"}," in the rooms for which they were originally ",{"n":5,"options":["instructed","intended","performed","guarded"],"answer":"intended","answers":["intended"],"explanation":"B - intended. 'Intended' here means planned, designed for, not left to chance.","skill":"Part 1"},". The official residences are in regular use and the style and manner in which they are shown to visitors reflects their working status. Rooms are kept as close to their normal ",{"n":6,"options":["feature","location","destination","appearance"],"answer":"appearance","answers":["appearance"],"explanation":"D - appearance. Visually, the rooms are almost unchanged. Appearance is how something looks, the visual part.","skill":"Part 1"}," as possible. Inevitably, opening times are subject to change at short notice depending on circumstances. The Royal Collection, which is owned by the Queen as Sovereign in trust for her successors and the Nation, is administered by the Royal Collection Trust to which a proportion of the admission fee and other ",{"n":7,"options":["salary","budget","income","wage"],"answer":"income","answers":["income"],"explanation":"C - income. Income is a general term that is used here for money, coming from various sources. In this case - the visiting public.","skill":"Part 1"}," from visitors is directed. The remainder of this money funds the majority of the cost of restoring Windsor Castle which was badly ",{"n":8,"options":["destroyed","ruined","damaged","collapsed"],"answer":"damaged","answers":["damaged"],"explanation":"C - damaged. The only word that collocates with the adverb 'badly'. 'Destroyed' and 'ruined' are so-called 'extreme adjectives', meaning that they already express the final form - both mean complete destruction or rendering something completely useless.","skill":"Part 1"}," by fire in November 1992."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Inspiration","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["I have had what, I think, is the 0 _____ extraordinary day of my life. While the events are ",{"n":9,"answer":"still","answers":["still"],"explanation":"still. The conjunction 'while' hints at an adverb of time.","skill":"Part 2"}," clear in my mind, I wish to write them down. Let me introduce ",{"n":10,"answer":"myself","answers":["myself"],"explanation":"myself. The beginning of the next paragraph is the key - the author is telling us about himself, a self- introduction.","skill":"Part 2"},".  My name is Lawrence Terrel. I am thirty-five years old, and in perfect health. I have never been ill in my life, not even for a day. I am an artist. I am ",{"n":11,"answer":"not","answers":["not"],"explanation":"not. The contrast suggested by 'but' further in the sentence suggests that we need a conjunction - 'not very successful, but...'.","skill":"Part 2"}," very successful, but I earn enough money to ",{"n":12,"answer":"take","answers":["take"],"explanation":"take. 'Take care of' is a common phrase meaning 'to address a problem', 'to deal with' or 'to look after'.","skill":"Part 2"}," care of my needs. My only near relative, a sister, ",{"n":13,"answer":"died","answers":["died"],"explanation":"died. The next sentence goes 'So I have no family' which explains what happened to his sister.","skill":"Part 2"}," three years ago. So I have no family. I ate breakfast this morning at eight. After I had read the morning paper, I smoked my pipe and let my mind wander. I hoped I would think of ",{"n":14,"answer":"something","answers":["something"],"explanation":"something. He was looking for inspiration, for something to draw. 'Anything' shouldn't be used here as it is mostly found in negative and interrogative sentences (i. e. questions).","skill":"Part 2"}," to draw. The room was very hot, even ",{"n":15,"answer":"though","answers":["though","when"],"explanation":"though/when. Although the meaning changes depending on the choice, both words are acceptable.","skill":"Part 2"}," the door and window were open. I had decided to go to the public swimming pool when an idea for a drawing came to me. I began to ",{"n":16,"answer":"draw","answers":["draw","work","sketch"],"explanation":"draw/work/sketch. For the sake of avoiding repetition, I wouldn't recommend using 'work'. Of course, it isn't going to affect your mark.","skill":"Part 2"},". I was so interested in my work that I forgot to eat lunch. I did not stop until the clock struck five. I looked at what I had done. For a hurried picture, I felt it was the best thing I had ever drawn."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["People in the world of 0 _____ have to be very ",{"n":17,"answer":"observant","answers":["observant"],"explanation":"observant. Observant means attentive, good at seeing or noticing things and details. 'Observing' is wrong as it means 'watching, looking' and doesn't have the required meaning.","skill":"Part 3","base":"OBSERVE"}," in the way that they dress. It's an ",{"n":18,"answer":"irrefutable","answers":["irrefutable"],"explanation":"irrefutable. Irrefutable means 'impossible to argue with, true'. Note the indefinite article 'an' before the gaps that indicates that the word should start with a vowel sound. Mind the spelling.","skill":"Part 3","base":"REFUTE"}," fact that image is more than just a case of ",{"n":19,"answer":"decoration","answers":["decoration"],"explanation":"decoration. Decoration here is used in the meaning of something visual, having no function or practical usage.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DECORATE"}," for a celebrity. How they present themselves is all part of their artistic personality. It would be ",{"n":20,"answer":"foolish","answers":["foolish"],"explanation":"foolish. An adjective meaning unwise, not smart.","skill":"Part 3","base":"FOOL"}," though to think that somebody can be a successful celebrity just because of the clothes they wear. They don't ",{"n":21,"answer":"necessarily","answers":["necessarily"],"explanation":"necessarily. Make sure to get the spelling right, or you won't get a point for this question. One 'c', double 's'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"NECESSARY"}," have to wear ",{"n":22,"answer":"flashy","answers":["flashy"],"explanation":"flashy. Noun to adjective transformation, flashy means bright, catching attention.","skill":"Part 3","base":"FLASH"}," clothing but they do need to be talented and communicative and they also need to be ",{"n":23,"answer":"appreciative","answers":["appreciative"],"explanation":"appreciative. Note the preposition 'of'. To be appreciative of something or someone is to be grateful for it and acknowledge its importance.","skill":"Part 3","base":"APPRECIATE"}," of their fans who make them successful. They also need to be ",{"n":24,"answer":"adaptable","answers":["adaptable"],"explanation":"adaptable. Adaptable means ready and able to change according to the situation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ADAPT"}," so that they can cope with all the public attention."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"My parents last spoke to me a month ago.","keyword":"HEARD","secondBefore":"I ","secondAfter":" my parents for a month.","answers":["haven't heard from"],"display":"haven't heard from","explanation":"haven't heard from. Present perfect is needed here to indicate that the speaker still hasn't got in touch with their parents.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"All the students had a lot of respect for their teacher.","keyword":"LOOKED","secondBefore":"All the students ","secondAfter":" their teacher.","answers":["looked up to"],"display":"looked up to","explanation":"looked up to. 'To look up to someone' means to respect someone, to hold someone in high regard.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"Linda didn't feel like eating.","keyword":"MOOD","secondBefore":"Linda wasn't ","secondAfter":" eat.","answers":["in the mood to"],"display":"in the mood to","explanation":"in the mood to. 'To be in the mood for something/to do something' is to feel like doing something, to want to do something.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"My brother was treated unfairly by his teachers.","keyword":"DESERVE","secondBefore":"My brother ","secondAfter":" treated that way by his teachers.","answers":["didn't deserve to be"],"display":"didn't deserve to be","explanation":"didn't deserve to be. Positive into negative change.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"He couldn't buy the car he wanted because he didn't have enough money.","keyword":"TOO","secondBefore":"The car he wanted was ","secondAfter":" buy.","answers":["too expensive for him to"],"display":"too expensive for him to","explanation":"too expensive for him to. It is important to include 'for him', as it is he who couldn't afford the car. You have to be specific and keep the meaning of the original sentence unchanged, otherwise you lose one of two points.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"We all agreed with my father's proposal apart from Joy.","keyword":"EXCEPTION","secondBefore":"With ","secondAfter":" , we all agreed with my father's proposal.","answers":["the exception of Joy"],"display":"the exception of Joy","explanation":"the exception of Joy. 'With the exception of someone'.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-15","examNumber":15,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 05","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"15 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 05","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · New Cycling Schemes","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["The County Council has 0 _____ to give a higher ",{"n":1,"options":["advantage","income","benefit","priority"],"answer":"priority","answers":["priority"],"explanation":"D - priority. This is the only word that collocates with 'higher' here and fits in context.","skill":"Part 1"}," to cycling and agreed a new strategy to guide the way ahead in East Sussex. Cycling is a ",{"n":2,"options":["shortcut","high-speed","dangerous","low-cost"],"answer":"low-cost","answers":["low-cost"],"explanation":"D - low-cost. The paragraph mentions the benefits of cycling. 'Shortcut' is not an adjective here and 'high- speed' shouldn't be mentioned, as the sentence that comes next mentions safety.","skill":"Part 1"},", healthy and environmentally-friendly form of transport. It is intended to make it safer, more convenient and attractive, and to increase the ",{"n":3,"options":["size","proportion","provision","proposal"],"answer":"proportion","answers":["proportion"],"explanation":"B - proportion. The overall share of people using bicycles in comparison with cars.","skill":"Part 1"}," of journeys made by bicycle. Cycling is being encouraged both for utility purposes (such as journeys to work, school and the shops) and for ",{"n":4,"options":["recreational","useless","sufficient","promotional"],"answer":"recreational","answers":["recreational"],"explanation":"A - recreational. The word relates to doing something pleasant in your free time. Other words do not fit this context.","skill":"Part 1"}," trips for exercise and enjoyment, including longer trips by tourists. Recent cycle schemes carried out in conjunction with District Councils and other bodies include the Brighton and Hove seafront route and the Cross Levels Way cycle route, in Eastbourne. ",{"n":5,"options":["Abnormal","Proper","Country","Local"],"answer":"Local","answers":["Local"],"explanation":"D - Local. People living in the area that is going to have the new cycling strategy implemented.","skill":"Part 1"}," people will be consulted as the strategy is implemented. The County Council will work with local cycling and other groups, and a countywide Cycling Forum will be formed to ",{"n":6,"options":["improve","ensure","assert","maintain"],"answer":"ensure","answers":["ensure"],"explanation":"B - ensure. To make sure, to make certain.","skill":"Part 1"}," that all bodies concerned with cycling are in regular ",{"n":7,"options":["contact","basis","touch","account"],"answer":"contact","answers":["contact"],"explanation":"A - contact. 'To be in contact' is to maintain communication between two sides. 'To be in touch' would work if it weren't for the adjective 'regular' – we can't say 'to be in regular touch'. The other two options do not fit.","skill":"Part 1"},". The objectives of the Cycling Strategy are given in a leaflet, and a ",{"n":8,"options":["copy","book","letter","network"],"answer":"copy","answers":["copy"],"explanation":"A - copy. A copy of the same document. It's neither a book nor a letter, but a set of rules they intend to implement.","skill":"Part 1"}," of the full strategy document can be seen in County Council Public Libraries."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Holidays with friends","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["I always 0 _____ to go to Porchester ",{"n":9,"answer":"for","answers":["for","during"],"explanation":"for/during. Both prepositions are acceptable here - both point at a period of time.","skill":"Part 2"}," my summer holidays. It is a quiet little town, full of old and interesting buildings. Very ",{"n":10,"answer":"few","answers":["few"],"explanation":"few. 'So there are no crowds' indicates that we should use a word with the meaning of low numbers. 'Little' is only used with uncountable and therefore shouldn't be put here next to 'people', which is countable.","skill":"Part 2"},"visitors ever go there, so there are no crowds. I enjoyed its sleepy atmosphere. I work in a  big city, so a holiday in Porchester was a complete change ",{"n":11,"answer":"from","answers":["from"],"explanation":"from. 'A change from something'","skill":"Part 2"}," my usual life. Besides, I found out about the history of the place. I wanted to learn how life used to be in Porchester - the stories of ",{"n":12,"answer":"its","answers":["its","the"],"explanation":"its/the. Both the determiner 'its' and the definite article 'the' have the same meaning in this context - to indicate that the people and the buildings we talk about belong to that place.","skill":"Part 2"}," people and buildings. I made notes on all these things ",{"n":13,"answer":"during","answers":["during","on"],"explanation":"during/on. Again, both words are acceptable as they have the same meaning of attributing some action to a period in time.","skill":"Part 2"}," my holidays and I soon knew more about the history of Porchester than most of the people ",{"n":14,"answer":"who","answers":["who","that"],"explanation":"who/that. Remember that we usually use 'who' to refer to people, even though 'that' is acceptable.","skill":"Part 2"}," lived there. I am not a rich man and I cannot afford to stay in hotels. When Jack Thompson heard that I wanted to spend my holidays in Porchester again all these years later, he invited me to stay with ",{"n":15,"answer":"him","answers":["him"],"explanation":"him. The pronoun here is used to refer to Jack.","skill":"Part 2"},". Jack and I were in the Army ",{"n":16,"answer":"together","answers":["together"],"explanation":"together. 'Be somewhere together'.","skill":"Part 2"}," during the war and we were good friends."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Mr Greaves had always been an 0 _____ man who should never have had a ",{"n":17,"answer":"managerial","answers":["managerial","management"],"explanation":"managerial/management. The adjective 'managerial' is rather tricky to spell, pay attention to it. Both options can be used here.","skill":"Part 3","base":"MANAGE"}," position. His bad temper was only just ",{"n":18,"answer":"bearable","answers":["bearable"],"explanation":"bearable. 'Was only just bearable' means 'almost unbearable, almost intolerable'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"BEAR"}," and there was often a ",{"n":19,"answer":"temptation","answers":["temptation"],"explanation":"temptation. A temptation is a desire to do something, often something wrong or unwise .","skill":"Part 3","base":"TEMPT"}," to tell him to sit down and relax. His office was chaotic with papers everywhere and had ",{"n":20,"answer":"absolutely","answers":["absolutely"],"explanation":"absolutely. An adverb is required here.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ABSOLUTE"}," no order to it. Once he went to a ",{"n":21,"answer":"conference","answers":["conference"],"explanation":"conference. The only difficulty here is to understand the link between the relatively unknown word 'to confer' (to exchange ideas) and the well-known 'conference'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CONFER"}," in Paris and on his ",{"n":22,"answer":"arrival","answers":["arrival"],"explanation":"arrival. A noun should be used here. Make sure to spell it with two 'r'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ARRIVE"}," at the hotel he completely forgot who he worked for when the ",{"n":23,"answer":"receptionist","answers":["receptionist"],"explanation":"receptionist. A person's occupation should be used here.","skill":"Part 3","base":"RECEIVE"}," asked him. When he tried to help he was a hindrance although his ",{"n":24,"answer":"intentions","answers":["intentions"],"explanation":"intentions. An intention is something you plan to do or make.","skill":"Part 3","base":"INTEND"}," were always good."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Michael's wife finds getting up early every morning difficult.","keyword":"USED","secondBefore":"Michael's wife ","secondAfter":" up early every morning.","answers":["isn't used to getting"],"display":"isn't used to getting","explanation":"isn't used to getting. 'Not used to doing something' means not in the habit of doing it.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"What a pity you didn't come to the party.","keyword":"WISH","secondBefore":"I ","secondAfter":" to the party.","answers":["wish you had come"],"display":"wish you had come","explanation":"wish you had come. Note that we have to use Past Perfect tense here with the 'I wish' construction.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"The burglar failed to enter the house through the first floor window.","keyword":"SUCCEED","secondBefore":"The burglar ","secondAfter":" the house through the first floor window.","answers":["didn't succeed in entering"],"display":"didn't succeed in entering","explanation":"didn't succeed in entering. 'Succeed in doing something' is used here in the negative as in the original sentence the burglars failed to get into the house.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"My hair needs to be cut before the wedding reception.","keyword":"MUST","secondBefore":"I ","secondAfter":" before the wedding reception.","answers":["must have my hair cut","must get my hair cut"],"display":"must have my hair cut","explanation":"must have/get my hair cut. Do not use the word 'haircut' as it would mean changing the original phrasing and possibly getting only one point out of two.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"\"Why didn't I ask her out last night?\" John said to himself.","keyword":"WONDERED","secondBefore":"John ","secondAfter":" asked her out the night before.","answers":["wondered why he hadn't"],"display":"wondered why he hadn't","explanation":"wondered why he hadn't. Reported speech is used here, which means changing Past Simple to Past Perfect according to the 'one step back' rule of tenses in reported speech.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"They think that their school teacher is a very rich man.","keyword":"BELIEVED","secondBefore":"Their school teacher ","secondAfter":" a very rich man.","answers":["is believed to be"],"display":"is believed to be","explanation":"is believed to be. A passive construction is used here, as suggested by the keyword 'believed'.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-16","examNumber":16,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 06","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"16 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 06","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · The Eighth Wonder of the World","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["The Thames Barrier is a 0 _____ part of the flood defence scheme for protecting London ",{"n":1,"options":["against","for","between","with"],"answer":"against","answers":["against"],"explanation":"A - against. 'To protect against something' is the only correct collocation in this context. 'To protect with something' would mean using something for protection, not protecting from something, in this case the rising water levels.","skill":"Part 1"}," rising water levels. The defenses ",{"n":2,"options":["and","also","still","too"],"answer":"also","answers":["also"],"explanation":"B - also. The only conjunction that fits grammatically. The other possible option could be: '...include raised river embankments and additional flood gates at strategic points too'.","skill":"Part 1"}," include raised river embankments and additional flood gates at strategic points, including the Barking Barrier. The unique structure that is the Barrier spans the 520-metre wide Woolwich reach and ",{"n":3,"options":["consists","includes","involves","contains"],"answer":"consists","answers":["consists"],"explanation":"A - consists. Here we limit our choice because of the preposition - 'consist' is the only verb in the list that can be followed by 'of'.","skill":"Part 1"}," of 10 separate movable gates, each pivoting and supported between concrete structures which house the operating machinery. When raised, the four main gates ",{"n":4,"options":["which","every","each","none"],"answer":"each","answers":["each"],"explanation":"C - each. 'Which' shouldn't be used here, as it would be introducing an additional clause, which is not present in the sentence.","skill":"Part 1"}," stand as high as a five storey building and as wide as the opening of Tower Bridge. Each ",{"n":5,"options":["measures","costs","calculates","weighs"],"answer":"weighs","answers":["weighs"],"explanation":"D - weighs. The mass of the gates is mentioned. This is not a very obvious verb and many overlook it. To weigh means to have a certain weight.","skill":"Part 1"}," 3700 tonnes. During the first twelve years of ",{"n":6,"options":["operation","surgery","vacation","profession"],"answer":"operation","answers":["operation"],"explanation":"A - operation. Being engaged in work or being active, functioning. Admittedly, the remaining choices students face here are rather weird.","skill":"Part 1"},", the Barrier has been closed twenty nine times to protect London. ",{"n":7,"options":["Sight","View","Hear","Explore"],"answer":"View","answers":["View"],"explanation":"B - View. Look at or see. 'Sight' can't be used as a verb. 'Hear' doesn't fit as you're unlikely to hear it - it makes no noise unless being lifted, which doesn't happen very often. 'Explore' doesn't make sense - you can't explore it sitting in a cafeteria.","skill":"Part 1"}," the Barrier from the comfortable cafeteria. Picnic on the riverside embankment. Enjoy beautiful views from the riverside walk. Visit the shop which stocks a large selection of souvenirs, books and Barrier information. There is a children's play area suitable for 4- to 12-year olds, located adjacent to the riverside walk. A visit to the spectacular Thames Barrier is a ",{"n":8,"options":["forgettable","memorable","forgetful","memorised"],"answer":"memorable","answers":["memorable"],"explanation":"B - memorable. Something important or exciting, something you're likely to remember. If something is memorised, it has been learned in advance in order to be used later, like a memorised answer to a question.","skill":"Part 1"}," experience."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Datamac","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["Datamac was the greatest machine 0 _____ the world. Its business ",{"n":9,"answer":"was","answers":["was"],"explanation":"was. The previous sentences suggest we are talking about the past. A verb 'to be' in past simple is all we need here.","skill":"Part 2"}," facts: it accepted ",{"n":10,"answer":"them","answers":["them"],"explanation":"them. The same construction with 'them' is used throughout the sentence: \"... accepted them, stored them, tested them... \"","skill":"Part 2"},", stored them, tested them and delivered them. It was located in Tokyo, where it received messages and questions from all ",{"n":11,"answer":"over","answers":["over"],"explanation":"over. \"From all over the world\" is a set phrase here.","skill":"Part 2"}," the world. It sent answers to every town and city in every ",{"n":12,"answer":"country","answers":["country","region"],"explanation":"country/region. Both words can be used here.","skill":"Part 2"},". An army of workers fed it with facts all the time. Other workers moved about inside Datamac and ",{"n":13,"answer":"looked","answers":["looked"],"explanation":"looked. 'To look after' here is to take care, to make sure it is in good working order.","skill":"Part 2"}," after it. Datamac had a very special duty. It was in ",{"n":14,"answer":"charge","answers":["charge","control","possession"],"explanation":"charge/control/possession. Was responsible for/controlled/had the facts.","skill":"Part 2"}," of all the facts about every person in the world. Each day it brought these four million facts together, and answered the question: \"",{"n":15,"answer":"What","answers":["What"],"explanation":"What. The introductory word for the question.","skill":"Part 2"}," will happen tomorrow?\" Every city in the world received report for its own part of the country. And the complete World Report ",{"n":16,"answer":"went","answers":["went"],"explanation":"went. 'Sent' would require a passive construction (was sent). Since the sentence is active, 'went' is correct.","skill":"Part 2"}," to the Chief of the Correction Force in Tokyo. John Williams had been Chief for only three weeks. By now he was not afraid of the World Report every morning. It was just a pile of papers, fifteen centimetres thick."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Being a DIY 0 _____ I was happy to take on the challenge of ",{"n":17,"answer":"modernising","answers":["modernising","modernizing"],"explanation":"modernising/modernizing. 'In charge of doing something'. The second variant of spelling (-zing) is chiefly American/Canadian English. It is recommended to be consistent and stick to only one variation of English throughout the test.","skill":"Part 3","base":"MODERN"}," an old but ",{"n":18,"answer":"delightful","answers":["delightful"],"explanation":"delightful. An adjective with a positive meaning, suggested by 'old, but ...' construction.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DELIGHT"}," cottage. I had been given the ",{"n":19,"answer":"invitation","answers":["invitation"],"explanation":"invitation. Make sure to spell the word right.","skill":"Part 3","base":"INVITE"}," by a man who used to be an ",{"n":20,"answer":"employee","answers":["employee"],"explanation":"employee. Not an 'employer', as the man worked for the author, not the other way round.","skill":"Part 3","base":"EMPLOY"}," of mine before he stopped working for me. The cottage needed to be changed ",{"n":21,"answer":"extensively","answers":["extensively"],"explanation":"extensively. An adverb is required here, pay attention to your spelling.","skill":"Part 3","base":"EXTEND"},", and my friend had to remove all his ",{"n":22,"answer":"possessions","answers":["possessions"],"explanation":"possessions. Another tricky to spell word, two double 's'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"POSSESS"}," before I could start. The requirements for such a task are many and before any ",{"n":23,"answer":"arrangements","answers":["arrangements"],"explanation":"arrangements. More words with difficult spelling, this time a noun. 'To make arrangements' is to prepare something that is needed in order to do something.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ARRANGE"}," could be made some organisation was needed. The final ",{"n":24,"answer":"transformation","answers":["transformation"],"explanation":"transformation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"TRANSFORM"}," was worth the effort though."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"The hairdresser did Mary's hair last Tuesday.","keyword":"HAD","secondBefore":"Mary ","secondAfter":" last Tuesday.","answers":["had her hair done"],"display":"had her hair done","explanation":"had her hair done. The first 'had' refers to the passive voice, the second one - to past simple ('last Tuesday').","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"Andrew's job is to supervise all the employees of the company.","keyword":"RESPONSIBLE","secondBefore":"Andrew ","secondAfter":" all the employees of the company.","answers":["is responsible for supervising"],"display":"is responsible for supervising","explanation":"is responsible for supervising. 'To be responsible for doing something' - to have the duty to do something.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"Harry reads faster than his little sister.","keyword":"READER","secondBefore":"Harry ","secondAfter":" his little sister.","answers":["is a faster reader than"],"display":"is a faster reader than","explanation":"is a faster reader than. A comparison is needed here, do not forget to use the indefinite article.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"\"I didn't steal the lady's purse\", said the boy.","keyword":"DENIED","secondBefore":"The boy ","secondAfter":" the lady's purse.","answers":["denied stealing stolen","denied having stolen"],"display":"denied stealing stolen","explanation":"denied stealing/having stolen. Do not forget that the limit on words is as low as two, so 'denied stealing' is acceptable.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"It was our first trip to Italy so we were very excited about it.","keyword":"TIME","secondBefore":"It was the ","secondAfter":" to Italy so we were very excited about it.","answers":["first time we had travelled","first time we had been"],"display":"first time we had travelled","explanation":"first time we had travelled/been. Past Perfect here is required by the 'It was the first/second/only time + Past Perfect' structure. The idea is that we refer back to an earlier experience before that past moment. It goes like this: First: we had never been to Italy before that moment and second: we had never been to Italy before that moment.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"\"Whose car is this?\" he asked.","keyword":"BELONG","secondBefore":"\"Who ","secondAfter":" ?\" he asked.","answers":["does this car belong to"],"display":"does this car belong to","explanation":"does this car belong to. \"to belong to someone\".","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-17","examNumber":17,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 07","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"17 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 07","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Space junk","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["The Space Age began 0 _____ over half a century ago, and ever since then the area just ",{"n":1,"options":["along","away","out","beyond"],"answer":"beyond","answers":["beyond"],"explanation":"D - beyond. 'just beyond' here means 'right outside, in the immediate vicinity on the outside'.","skill":"Part 1"}," the Earth's atmosphere has been filling up with all kinds of man-made objects that have become ",{"n":2,"options":["referred","known","called","named"],"answer":"known","answers":["known"],"explanation":"B - known. 'Become known as' is the correct collocation. 'Become called' is not used in this way","skill":"Part 1"}," as 'space junk'. The items up there ",{"n":3,"options":["include","enclose","cover","range"],"answer":"range","answers":["range"],"explanation":"D - range. 'Range from ... to' phrase is used to refer to the spectrum or variety of something, space junk in this case.","skill":"Part 1"}," from old satellites and parts of rockets to hundreds of thousands of pieces smaller than one centimetre, all of them travelling at extremely ",{"n":4,"options":["high","rapid","light","fast"],"answer":"high","answers":["high"],"explanation":"A - high. 'High speed' is the accepted collocation. Just like how prices are 'high' and 'low', rather than 'expensive' and 'cheap', speed can't be 'fast' or 'slow'.","skill":"Part 1"}," speed. Over the last five years, the number of such objects in space is ",{"n":5,"options":["assessed","supposed","estimated","regarded"],"answer":"estimated","answers":["estimated"],"explanation":"C - estimated. 'To estimate' is to guess or calculate something, such as price, amount and so on.","skill":"Part 1"}," to have risen by 50 per cent, and this has ",{"n":6,"options":["largely","greatly","importantly","absolutely"],"answer":"greatly","answers":["greatly"],"explanation":"B - greatly. By a considerable amount. 'Largely' in this context would have meant 'generally', so it shouldn't be used.","skill":"Part 1"}," increased the risk of damage to working satellites or space vehicles with crews on board. International agreement has therefore now been ",{"n":7,"options":["arrived","reached","finished","closed"],"answer":"reached","answers":["reached"],"explanation":"B - reached. 'To reach an agreement' is another strong collocation. Other words do not form any.","skill":"Part 1"}," on limiting the amount of new space junk. Scientists have also ",{"n":8,"options":["caught up with","put up with","come up with","kept up with"],"answer":"come up with","answers":["come up with"],"explanation":"C - come up with. To invent, to suggest an idea or a plan. 'Caught up with' and 'kept up with' have a similar meaning of reaching something you're late for, e.g. something you're falling behind. 'To put up with' means to accept something unpleasant or undesirable.","skill":"Part 1"}," some interesting suggestions for tidying up space. These include using laser beams, giant nets and even an enormous umbrella-like device to collect tiny bits of junk."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Chewing gum","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["We still tend 0 _____ think chewing gum is a fairly recent invention, even ",{"n":9,"answer":"though","answers":["though","when"],"explanation":"though/when. 'Though' is used to contrast two ideas in the sentence. We can also use 'when' to connect two ideas, although with lesser contrasting. Admittedly, the first option is much more fitting, but FCE is pretty forgiving in this regard.","skill":"Part 2"}," there is evidence it was used 5,000 years ago in Finland. The Ancient Greeks also chewed gum, as ",{"n":10,"answer":"did","answers":["did"],"explanation":"did. \"As did\" is used to avoid saying \"the Aztecs chewed gum\". The meaning is the same to the construction \"So did\". Ultimately, it helps us avoid unnecessary word repetition.","skill":"Part 2"},"the Aztecs in Mexico during the sixteenth century. As far as we know, however, it wasn't ",{"n":11,"answer":"until","answers":["until","till"],"explanation":"until/till. \"It wasn't until\" indicated a period of time that marked the beginning of chewing gum popularity as we know it. 'Till' can also be used, the less formal option.","skill":"Part 2"}," 1869 that chewing gum became popular in its present form, ",{"n":12,"answer":"when","answers":["when"],"explanation":"when. A reference to the previous date is made, clarifying what happened in that year.","skill":"Part 2"}," a New York inventor called Thomas Adams first had the idea of adding flavour to it. Nowadays, of course, it is chewed around the world, ",{"n":13,"answer":"despite","answers":["despite"],"explanation":"despite. Another contrast is introduced by \"despite the fact\" construction.","skill":"Part 2"}," the fact that it continues to be regarded by some ",{"n":14,"answer":"as","answers":["as"],"explanation":"as. \"To regard as\" has the same meaning as \"to believe, to consider\".","skill":"Part 2"}," an unpleasant habit. Unfortunately, far too many people drop used gum onto the pavement, ",{"n":15,"answer":"where","answers":["where"],"explanation":"where. 'Where' here refers to the pavement.","skill":"Part 2"}," it  remains for some time because it is extremely difficult to remove once it has stuck to the surface. On the other hand, those ",{"n":16,"answer":"in","answers":["in"],"explanation":"in. \"To be in favour of something\" means to be for it, to support it.","skill":"Part 2"}," favour of chewing gum claim it helps them relax, improves their concentration, and helps keep their teeth clean."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Remembering people's names","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Most of us have suffered the 0 _____ of forgetting someone's name. Often we fail to pay attention when ",{"n":17,"answer":"introductions","answers":["introductions"],"explanation":"introductions. The plural form of the noun is required, as suggested by the plural form 'are' after the gap.","skill":"Part 3","base":"INTRODUCE"}," are made, but later on in the conversation we don't want to appear ",{"n":18,"answer":"impolite","answers":["impolite"],"explanation":"impolite. The negative form of the adjective is required, the context helps us understand that.","skill":"Part 3","base":"POLITE"}," by asking them what their name is. Fortunately, there are some simple ways you can ",{"n":19,"answer":"overcome","answers":["overcome"],"explanation":"overcome. The meaning of the word is 'to solve, to deal with a problem'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"COME"}," this problem. One is to improve your powers of ",{"n":20,"answer":"observation","answers":["observation"],"explanation":"observation. Do not use the gerund 'observing'; generally it is advised not to use -ing if a better form can be made.","skill":"Part 3","base":"OBSERVE"},". Practise studying faces in public places, making a mental note of physical ",{"n":21,"answer":"characteristics","answers":["characteristics"],"explanation":"characteristics. Another plural form should be used here - the author is giving several examples such as foreheads and eyebrows.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CHARACTER"}," such as high foreheads or narrow eyebrows. You'll be surprised what a wide ",{"n":22,"answer":"variety","answers":["variety"],"explanation":"variety. A characteristic of being different. If there's a variety of something, it means that it comes in many different forms, shapes or colours.","skill":"Part 3","base":"VARY"}," of shapes and sizes people's features have. Then, when you first meet someone, remember them as 'Laura with the small nose', for example. With surnames, make ",{"n":23,"answer":"visual","answers":["visual"],"explanation":"visual. The following noun suggests that we should be using an adjective.","skill":"Part 3","base":"VISION"}," associations. For instance, imagine people called Cook, Ford or King making a meal, driving a car or wearing a crown, respectively. Finally, ending with the person's name, as in 'See you later, Max' is a good way of ",{"n":24,"answer":"ensuring","answers":["ensuring"],"explanation":"ensuring. Good way of doing something. Use the en- prefix, as 'insuring' has a different meaning.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SURE"}," that you don't forget it."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Thomas spoke so quickly I had difficulty understanding him.","keyword":"IT","secondBefore":"Thomas spoke so quickly I ","secondAfter":" him.","answers":["found it difficult to understand"],"display":"found it difficult to understand","explanation":"found it difficult to understand. 'To find something easy/difficult to do'","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"They've postponed the match and it'll be played next weekend.","keyword":"PUT","secondBefore":"The match ","secondAfter":" until next weekend.","answers":["has been put off"],"display":"has been put off","explanation":"has been put off. 'To put off' means to postpone, to move to a later date. Note that you have to use Present Perfect as in the original sentence to get full points.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"I'm never going to speak to Louis again.","keyword":"INTENTION","secondBefore":"I've got ","secondAfter":" to Louis again.","answers":["no intention of (ever) speaking"],"display":"no intention of (ever) speaking","explanation":"no intention of (ever) speaking. 'To have (no) intention' means to be (not) willing or planning to do something.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"It seems certain that lightning started the forest fire.","keyword":"HAVE","secondBefore":"The forest fire ","secondAfter":" by lightning.","answers":["must have been started"],"display":"must have been started","explanation":"must have been started. A passive voice structure is required here - suggested by the keyword 'have'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"It wasn't worth going to the market because it was closing.","keyword":"POINT","secondBefore":"The market was closing, so ","secondAfter":" going there.","answers":["there was no point in"],"display":"there was no point in","explanation":"there was no point in. If there is no point in something, it is not worth doing.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"We phoned the restaurant, but they said booking a table wasn't necessary.","keyword":"HAVE","secondBefore":"We phoned the restaurant, but they said we ","secondAfter":" a table.","answers":["didn't have to book"],"display":"didn't have to book","explanation":"didn't have to book. 'Didn't have to do something' - both sentences contain the negative form of the verb. There was no need to book a table.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-18","examNumber":18,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 08","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"18 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 08","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Planes can make it rain","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["Living next to an airport has always meant having to 0 _____ the noise of planes landing and taking off. Now it seems that ",{"n":1,"options":["neighbouring","close","local","surrounded"],"answer":"local","answers":["local"],"explanation":"C- local. 'Local residents' makes the only strong collocation of the four.","skill":"Part 1"}," residents also have bad weather, according to a ",{"n":2,"options":["lately","recently","lastly","freshly"],"answer":"recently","answers":["recently"],"explanation":"B - recently. A recently published study. 'Lately' is more common for events that happen again and again, e.g. 'Mike has been visiting us less often lately'.","skill":"Part 1"}," published study. The scientists looked at satellite ",{"n":3,"options":["images","figures","aspects","portraits"],"answer":"images","answers":["images"],"explanation":"A- images. A satellite image is a picture taken from the orbit of our planet.","skill":"Part 1"}," of clouds above airports, and also studied computer models of the way clouds behave. What they found was that as a plane flies through a very cold cloud, the air behind it expands and then cools ",{"n":4,"options":["totally","extremely","entirely","perfectly"],"answer":"extremely","answers":["extremely"],"explanation":"B - extremely. The only word having the meaning of 'very rapidly'.","skill":"Part 1"}," rapidly. This sudden drop in temperature, ",{"n":5,"options":["joined","attached","composed","combined"],"answer":"combined","answers":["combined"],"explanation":"D- combined. The only word of the four that can be used with 'of'. If something is combined with something else, they are joined together.","skill":"Part 1"}," with the hole formed in the cloud where the plane has passed through, can increase the ",{"n":6,"options":["forecasts","certainties","chances","opportunities"],"answer":"chances","answers":["chances"],"explanation":"C - chances. To increase chances of something is to make something more likely to happen.","skill":"Part 1"}," of rain or snow on the ground. In the case of major airports, with hundreds of flights every day, this can have a significant ",{"n":7,"options":["result","influence","consequence","impression"],"answer":"influence","answers":["influence"],"explanation":"B - influence. Both 'influence' and 'impression' work well with the preposition 'on', but naturally you can't make an impression on weather. You can, however, influence it.","skill":"Part 1"}," on weather patterns up to 100 kilometres away. The researchers point out, however, that aircraft passing through clouds are ",{"n":8,"options":["doubtful","unsure","unlikely","improbable"],"answer":"unlikely","answers":["unlikely"],"explanation":"C - unlikely. Unlikely to do something. 'Doubtful' is not used with the 'to' infinitive; 'Unsure' doesn't fit grammatically; 'Improbable' doesn't collocate well with 'affect'. In other words, we never say 'improbable to affect something'.","skill":"Part 1"}," to affect the global climate."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Cycling makes financial sense","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["These days, most people are aware 0 _____ the environmental impact of motor vehicles, and they realise that cars are responsible ",{"n":9,"answer":"for","answers":["for"],"explanation":"for. 'To be responsible for something' means to be the reason that something has happened, especially something bad.","skill":"Part 2"}," many of the problems facing their cities. Not ",{"n":10,"answer":"many","answers":["many"],"explanation":"many. 'Not many people' is a fixed quantifier, that effectively means 'few people'. It contrasts with the previous sentence suggesting that people are aware of environmental issues.","skill":"Part 2"}," people, however, ever stop ",{"n":11,"answer":"to","answers":["to","and"],"explanation":"to/and. Both options can be used with slightly different meaning: 'Stop to think' is to stop in order to spend some time thinking about it; 'Stop and think' are two actions followed by one another.","skill":"Part 2"}," think about how much money they would save by switching from driving to cycling. Cars cost a lot to buy, and ",{"n":12,"answer":"unlike","answers":["unlike"],"explanation":"unlike. A contrast is made between large investments - houses and cars.","skill":"Part 2"}," other major investments such as houses, they quickly lose their value. On ",{"n":13,"answer":"top","answers":["top"],"explanation":"top. 'On top of that' is a set expression meaning 'in addition to something'.","skill":"Part 2"}," of that, there are running costs such as repair bills, tax and insurance, all of ",{"n":14,"answer":"which","answers":["which"],"explanation":"which. A relative clause that requires the usage of which (and not that).","skill":"Part 2"}," seem to go up every year.  Bicycles, by contrast, are far ",{"n":15,"answer":"less","answers":["less"],"explanation":"less. Making a comparison with the topic of the previous paragraph - cars - we can deduce, that the word 'less' (and not 'more') is required here.","skill":"Part 2"}," expensive to purchase and maintain, do not require petrol and are easy to park. And if a lot more people took up cycling, the government could spend less of the nation's resources on roads, which at present their taxes help pay for ",{"n":16,"answer":"whether","answers":["whether"],"explanation":"whether. 'Whether or not' means 'unimportant which of the two options is true'.","skill":"Part 2"}," they drive on them or not."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Too many emails","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Some years ago, there were 0 _____ that email would soon be replaced as the most common means of online communication by social ",{"n":17,"answer":"network","answers":["network","networking"],"explanation":"network/networking. Either is acceptable. While 'social network' focuses on the type of website, 'social networking' is more about what kind of activity happens on that website. Small differences, but still worth noting. Don't be fooled into answering 'social worker' - although there might be websites like this, it is definitely not the focus of this text.","skill":"Part 3","base":"WORK"}," sites. Since then, however, the number of emails sent has increased ",{"n":18,"answer":"steadily","answers":["steadily"],"explanation":"steadily. Adverb that means 'at an even rate', gradually but surely.","skill":"Part 3","base":"STEADY"},", to the point that the volume received on a daily basis has become ",{"n":19,"answer":"stressful","answers":["stressful"],"explanation":"stressful. An adjective that denotes stress is needed. Note the ending '-ful' with only one 'l'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"STRESS"}," for many people. For some users, the situation has become so ",{"n":20,"answer":"unbearable","answers":["unbearable"],"explanation":"unbearable. An adjective with a negative meaning is required. Make sure to spell the word right.","skill":"Part 3","base":"BEAR"}," that they regularly delete all the emails they receive without even bothering to read them, which is rather ",{"n":21,"answer":"risky","answers":["risky"],"explanation":"risky. An adjective with the meaning of risk.","skill":"Part 3","base":"RISK"},", to say the least. A less extreme measure is to install software that sorts incoming mail into different categories. By doing this, promotions from ",{"n":22,"answer":"commercial","answers":["commercial"],"explanation":"commercial. Do not forget to spell it with double 'm', otherwise you won't get the point for this question.","skill":"Part 3","base":"COMMERCE"}," organisations, for instance, go into a separate folder from messages arriving from friends or employers, ",{"n":23,"answer":"enabling","answers":["enabling"],"explanation":"enabling. A prefix 'en-' is added as well as the ending '-ing' to form a participle form that we need to link the two clauses.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ABLE"}," us to see which emails are more important than others. Not all of us, though, may be so keen to let software make such ",{"n":24,"answer":"decisions","answers":["decisions"],"explanation":"decisions. A plural form is needed as we are not talking about one email, but many of them.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DECIDE"}," for us."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"'I'll take you to the station if you're ready,' my brother said.","keyword":"LONG","secondBefore":"My brother said he'd take me to the station ","secondAfter":" ready.","answers":["as long as I was","so long as I was"],"display":"as long as I was","explanation":"as/so long as I was. Remember that in reported speech we take one step back with all the tenses (as I was...).","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"My bike needs repairing again.","keyword":"GET","secondBefore":"I'll have to ","secondAfter":" again.","answers":["get my old bike repaired"],"display":"get my old bike repaired","explanation":"get my old bike repaired. Passive voice is used here - as suggested by the keyword 'get'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"The firm said profits had fallen on account of the recession.","keyword":"FALL","secondBefore":"The firm blamed ","secondAfter":" the recession.","answers":["the fall in profits on"],"display":"the fall in profits on","explanation":"the fall in profits on. 'To blame on something or someone'. The main difficulty is using the right preposition.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"Sigourney regretted spending so much in the shops.","keyword":"WISHED","secondBefore":"Sigourney ","secondAfter":" less in the shops.","answers":["wished (that) she had spent","wished (that) she she'd spent"],"display":"wished (that) she had spent","explanation":"wished (that) she had/she'd spent. Past Perfect is needed here to show what happened first and what followed it. In this case first she spent money, then she regretted.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"People believe that poem was written about these beautiful hills.","keyword":"HAVE","secondBefore":"That poem ","secondAfter":" written about these beautiful hills.","answers":["is believed to have been"],"display":"is believed to have been","explanation":"is believed to have been. Another passive voice construction. Make sure to spell 'believed' right.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"Without your help, I wouldn't have finished the job.","keyword":"BEEN","secondBefore":"I wouldn't have finished the job if ","secondAfter":" your help.","answers":["it had not been for"],"display":"it had not been for","explanation":"it had not been for. Another Past Perfect tense example to show relation of actions in time.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-19","examNumber":19,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 09","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"19 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 09","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Checking your mobile phone","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["How often do you check your phone? For many of us, our phone is an essential 0 _____of everyday life, but apparently we are spending an increasing amount of time checking it for information without being ",{"n":1,"options":["sensitive","cautious","sensible","conscious"],"answer":"conscious","answers":["conscious"],"explanation":"D - conscious. To be conscious of something is to be aware of it, to realise and understand what you do.","skill":"Part 1"}," of doing so. Research in Helsinki shows that phone checking ",{"n":2,"options":["virtually","typically","widely","suitably"],"answer":"typically","answers":["typically"],"explanation":"B - typically. Normally, usually, as a rule. 'Virtually' is normally used to mean 'almost all' or 'almost none'.","skill":"Part 1"}," lasts less than 30 seconds on each occasion, and usually ",{"n":3,"options":["consists","composes","involves","includes"],"answer":"consists","answers":["consists"],"explanation":"A - consists. The only verb here that forms a strong collocation with the 'of' preposition.","skill":"Part 1"}," of opening a single application such as social media. The study also found many users check their mobiles throughout the ",{"n":4,"options":["complete","total","normal","entire"],"answer":"entire","answers":["entire"],"explanation":"D - entire. Whole, all day. A 'normal day' could work grammatically, but it would require rearranging the sentence, which is not allowed in FCE Use of English Part 1.","skill":"Part 1"}," day, and that what they check is often ",{"n":5,"options":["attached","assumed","associated","accompanied"],"answer":"associated","answers":["associated"],"explanation":"C - associated. Associated with here means 'connected with, related to'; 'accompanied with' shouldn't be used because the meaning would change to 'joined by'.","skill":"Part 1"}," with particular contexts. For instance, when travelling to work or college, people tend to check their email; if they are bored, they get a quick ",{"n":6,"options":["update","revision","review","upgrade"],"answer":"update","answers":["update"],"explanation":"A - update. This is the only noun that takes the preposition 'on'. The others commonly take the preposition 'of'.","skill":"Part 1"}," on the latest news. Checking this frequently can easily become a habit, which some say can ",{"n":7,"options":["disturb","distract","disrupt","distribute"],"answer":"distract","answers":["distract"],"explanation":"B - distract. To distract is to take somebody's attention away from something.","skill":"Part 1"}," us from more important things. Others, though, believe that being able to ",{"n":8,"options":["capture","import","obtain","seize"],"answer":"obtain","answers":["obtain"],"explanation":"C - obtain. You can obtain information, but you can't capture, import or seize it. 'To obtain' basically is the same as 'to get'.","skill":"Part 1"}," so much new information so quickly makes life far more interesting."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · The Australian city of Perth","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["Perth, 0 _____ capital of the state of Western Australia, has been described as one of the most remote cities ",{"n":9,"answer":"on","answers":["on"],"explanation":"on. 'on Earth' - on the surface of our planet. Prepositions of place can be tricky, so make sure to revise them before the exam.","skill":"Part 2"}," Earth. Founded in 1829 and named after Perth in Scotland, it grew rapidly in the late nineteenth century ",{"n":10,"answer":"as","answers":["as"],"explanation":"as. 'As a result' is used in the meaning 'because, due to'.","skill":"Part 2"}," a result of the discovery of gold in the state. Nowadays it has a population of around two million people, and many of ",{"n":11,"answer":"them","answers":["them","these","those"],"explanation":"them/these/those. Any of the determiners can be used with virtually no change to the meaning of the sentence.","skill":"Part 2"}," were born outside Australia. This has led ",{"n":12,"answer":"to","answers":["to"],"explanation":"to. 'lead to something/to doing something' - to cause something, to make something happen.","skill":"Part 2"}," Perth becoming a culturally diverse city, in ",{"n":13,"answer":"spite","answers":["spite"],"explanation":"spite. 'In spite' is spelled in two words and followed by the 'of' preposition. Not to be confused with 'despite' which is one word and takes no prepositions: 'In spite of his success, he was never truly happy'; 'Despite the poor weather, we still enjoyed the weekend'.","skill":"Part 2"}," of the fact that the nearest large town is over 2,000 kilometres away, with well-established communities from southern Europe and South-East Asia ",{"n":14,"answer":"in","answers":["in"],"explanation":"in. 'In particular' here has the meaning 'especially, specifically'.","skill":"Part 2"}," particular. The city's five universities have also attracted students from ",{"n":15,"answer":"all","answers":["all"],"explanation":"all. 'From all over the world' - from everywhere, from all parts of the planet.","skill":"Part 2"}," over the world. Young people find much to enjoy in and around Perth on ",{"n":16,"answer":"account","answers":["account"],"explanation":"account. 'On account of something' - due to, because of something.","skill":"Part 2"}," of its outdoor lifestyle, which offers a wide range of activities including swimming at its many beaches, cycling both in town and countryside, and visiting nearby nature reserves."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Advertising in schools","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["In certain countries, there has been rapid 0 _____ in the spending power of teenagers. Realising this, firms are aiming even more of their ",{"n":17,"answer":"products","answers":["products"],"explanation":"products. Plural form of the noun is required here, as suggested by the context.","skill":"Part 3","base":"PRODUCE"}," at young people, and ",{"n":18,"answer":"increasingly","answers":["increasingly"],"explanation":"increasingly. To do something in a certain manner - an adverb is required here, formed by adding '-ly' ending to the word.","skill":"Part 3","base":"INCREASE"}," they are doing this by advertising directly in schools. These companies see children as the ",{"n":19,"answer":"consumers","answers":["consumers"],"explanation":"consumers. 'Children' is plural here and so should be 'consumers' - people who consume a product.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CONSUME"}," of the future, and hope that when they become adults they will maintain their ",{"n":20,"answer":"loyalty","answers":["loyalty"],"explanation":"loyalty. 'Loyalty to something' (in this case, certain commercial products) means choosing that particular brand in favour of all others because you've used it and liked it in the past.","skill":"Part 3","base":"LOYAL"}," to brands they first started buying in their youth. As a result, many ",{"n":21,"answer":"manufacturers","answers":["manufacturers"],"explanation":"manufacturers. Another plural form of the word suggested by the determiner 'many'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"MANUFACTURE"}," are keen to supply equipment to schools, sponsor sports activities or provide students with kit that carries their logo. Other firms install machines selling snacks and drinks, and these are often highly ",{"n":22,"answer":"profitable","answers":["profitable"],"explanation":"profitable. A common adverb + adjective combination, the adjective formed by adding the '-able' suffix.","skill":"Part 3","base":"PROFIT"}," for both the companies and the schools. There is, however, considerable ",{"n":23,"answer":"disagreement","answers":["disagreement"],"explanation":"disagreement. The context of the last paragraph suggests using the word in the negative, so we form a noun by adding a negative prefix 'dis-' and an ending '-ment'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"AGREE"}," about whether this is a good idea. Some claim these firms provide a useful service, but others argue that these machines encourage ",{"n":24,"answer":"unhealthy","answers":["unhealthy"],"explanation":"unhealthy. Another negative word, this time an adjective, formed by adding a negative prefix 'un-'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"HEALTH"}," eating habits at a time when many doctors are concerned about the diet of the young."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"The storm left very few trees standing.","keyword":"WERE","secondBefore":"Hardly ","secondAfter":" after the storm.","answers":["any trees were left standing","any trees were still standing"],"display":"any trees were left standing","explanation":"any trees were left/still standing. Inversion is used here, making the sentence a bit more difficult to get right. 'Hardly any' is the paraphrase of 'very few'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"'What's the height of that building?' I asked the guide.","keyword":"HOW","secondBefore":"I asked the guide ","secondAfter":" was.","answers":["how high that building"],"display":"how high that building","explanation":"how high that building. Even though we're using reported speech, 'that' isn't changed, as there is nothing to change it to.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"The new printer is very similar to the old model.","keyword":"MUCH","secondBefore":"There is not ","secondAfter":" the new printer and the old model.","answers":["much difference between"],"display":"much difference between","explanation":"much difference between. A comparison between two things is made, using the construction 'there is (not) much difference between'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"Simon wouldn't apologise for being so rude.","keyword":"WAS","secondBefore":"Simon refused to ","secondAfter":" for being so rude.","answers":["say (that) he was sorry"],"display":"say (that) he was sorry","explanation":"say (that) he was sorry. 'That' is optional here and can be left out.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"What attracts people so much to this city is its nightlife.","keyword":"FIND","secondBefore":"What people ","secondAfter":" this city is its nightlife.","answers":["find (so) attractive about"],"display":"find (so) attractive about","explanation":"find (so) attractive about. 'Attractive' should be used with 'about' and not 'in', a common mistake.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"It's unlikely our firm will manage to win the contract.","keyword":"SUCCEED","secondBefore":"Our firm is unlikely ","secondAfter":" the contract.","answers":["to succeed in winning"],"display":"to succeed in winning","explanation":"to succeed in winning. 'To succeed in doing something'.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-20","examNumber":20,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 10","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"20 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 10","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · What is genealogy","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["Genealogy is a 0 _____ of history. It concerns family history, ",{"n":1,"options":["instead","rather","except","sooner"],"answer":"rather","answers":["rather"],"explanation":"B - rather. 'Rather than' is used to contrast it with the second clause and means 'instead of, as opposed to'. 'Instead' shouldn't be picked as it is used with 'of' preposition.","skill":"Part 1"}," than the national or world history studied at school. It doesn't merely involve drawing a family tree, however - tracing your family history can also ",{"n":2,"options":["cause","mean","result","lead"],"answer":"result","answers":["result"],"explanation":"C - result. 'To result in something'. The only verb here that works with the 'in' preposition. 'Lead' fits lexically, however it should be used with 'to': 'Increased unemployment might lead to higher crime rates'.","skill":"Part 1"}," in learning about your roots and your identity. The internet enables millions of people worldwide to ",{"n":3,"options":["accomplish","access","approach","admit"],"answer":"access","answers":["access"],"explanation":"B - access. To get access, to use, to be allowed to use something.","skill":"Part 1"}," information about their family history, without great ",{"n":4,"options":["fee","price","charge","expense"],"answer":"expense","answers":["expense"],"explanation":"D - expense. The focus here is people who spend money, not the providers of such information, therefore 'charge' shouldn't be used.","skill":"Part 1"},". People who research their family history often ",{"n":5,"options":["describe","define","remark","regard"],"answer":"remark","answers":["remark"],"explanation":"C - remark. 'To remark' is to point out, to mention, to say what you notice.","skill":"Part 1"}," that it's a fascinating hobby which ",{"n":6,"options":["reveals","opens","begins","arises"],"answer":"reveals","answers":["reveals"],"explanation":"A - reveals. To reveal is to make known, to uncover, to learn something that was previously a mystery or a secret.","skill":"Part 1"}," a lot about where they come from and whether they have famous ancestors. According to a survey involving 900 people who had researched their family history, the chances of discovering a celebrity in your past are one in ten. The survey also concluded that the ",{"n":7,"options":["older","greater","higher","further"],"answer":"further","answers":["further"],"explanation":"D - further. The further back you go in your family history, the more likely you are to find something interesting.","skill":"Part 1"}," back you follow your family line, the more likely you are to find a relation who was much wealthier than you are. However, the vast majority of people who ",{"n":8,"options":["attended","participated","included","associated"],"answer":"participated","answers":["participated"],"explanation":"B - participated. To participate in something. 'Included' would need the passive here, so we can't choose it.","skill":"Part 1"}," in the survey discovered they were better off than their ancestors."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Motorbike stunt rider","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["I work 0 _____ a motorbike stunt rider - that is, I do tricks on my motorbike at shows. The Le Mans race track in France was ",{"n":9,"answer":"where","answers":["where"],"explanation":"where. The author goes back in time - using the 'where' conjunction.","skill":"Part 2"}," I first saw some guys doing motorbike stunts. I'd never seen anyone riding a motorbike using just the back wheel before and I was ",{"n":10,"answer":"so","answers":["so"],"explanation":"so. 'So… that' structure is used to show result: 'I was so impressed that…'","skill":"Part 2"}," impressed I went straight home and taught ",{"n":11,"answer":"myself","answers":["myself"],"explanation":"myself. The sentence that follows the gap makes it clear that the author is talking about himself.","skill":"Part 2"}," to do the same. It wasn't very long before I began to earn my living at shows performing my own motorbike stunts. I have a degree ",{"n":12,"answer":"in","answers":["in"],"explanation":"in. A degree in something - meaning that the person had that subject as their major at college or university.","skill":"Part 2"}," mechanical engineering; this helps me to look at the physics ",{"n":13,"answer":"which","answers":["which","that"],"explanation":"which/that. Both relative pronouns can be used here - they help us understand the meaning and connection in the sentence - what kind of physics exactly the author means here.","skill":"Part 2"}," lies behind each stunt. In addition to being responsible for design changes to the motorbike, I have to work ",{"n":14,"answer":"out","answers":["out","on","at"],"explanation":"out/on/at. Different prepositions change the meaning slightly. 'To work out' is to understand how something works. 'To work on' is to spend your time doing something, similarly to 'work at'.","skill":"Part 2"}," every stunt I do. People often think that my work is very dangerous, but, apart ",{"n":15,"answer":"from","answers":["from"],"explanation":"from. Apart from here has a similar meaning to 'other than'.","skill":"Part 2"}," some minor mechanical problem happening  occasionally during a stunt, nothing ever goes wrong. I never feel in ",{"n":16,"answer":"any","answers":["any"],"explanation":"any. The author doesn't feel any danger at all because of all the excitement. 'Some' shouldn't be used here because it would change the message - the author feels very confident, and 'any kind of danger' illustrates that.","skill":"Part 2"}," kind of danger because I'm very experienced."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · An incredible vegetable","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Garlic, a member of the Liliaceae family which also includes onions, is 0 _____ used in cooking all around the world. China is currently the largest ",{"n":17,"answer":"producer","answers":["producer"],"explanation":"producer. A noun-forming suffix '-er' is used.","skill":"Part 3","base":"PRODUCT"}," of garlic, which is particularly associated with the dishes of northern Africa and southern Europe. It is native to central Asia and has long had a history as a health giving food, used both to prevent and cure ",{"n":18,"answer":"illness(es)","answers":["illness(es)"],"explanation":"illness(es). Note that the word is spelled with double 'l' and double 's'. Both plural and singular forms are acceptable as they do not change the meaning.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ILL"}," In Ancient Egypt, workers building the pyramids were given garlic to keep them strong, while Olympic athletes in Greece ate it to increase their resistance to infection. The forefather of antibiotic medicine, Louis Pasteur, claimed garlic was as ",{"n":19,"answer":"effective","answers":["effective"],"explanation":"effective. A comparison is made using 'as... as...' structure.","skill":"Part 3","base":"EFFECT"}," as penicillin in treating infections. Modern-day ",{"n":20,"answer":"scientists","answers":["scientists"],"explanation":"scientists. The plural form is needed as the verb 'have' is used in plural. Another word with tricky spelling - make sure to get it right, otherwise it won't be scored.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SCIENCE"}," have proved that garlic can indeed kill bacteria and even some viruses, so it can be very useful for people who have coughs and colds. In ",{"n":21,"answer":"addition","answers":["addition"],"explanation":"addition. 'In addition' means 'what is more, also'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ADD"}," , some doctors believe that garlic can reduce blood ",{"n":22,"answer":"pressure","answers":["pressure"],"explanation":"pressure. 'Blood pressure' is a commonly used medical term, so this one shouldn't cause any difficulties.","skill":"Part 3","base":"PRESS"}," The only ",{"n":23,"answer":"disadvantage","answers":["disadvantage"],"explanation":"disadvantage. A negative prefix 'dis-' is used to change the meaning of the word to fit the context.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ADVANTAGE"}," to this truly amazing food is that the strong and rather ",{"n":24,"answer":"spicy","answers":["spicy"],"explanation":"spicy. The right adjective is formed by adding '-y' ending and dropping the letter 'e'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SPICE"}," smell of garlic is not the most pleasant!"]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Joan was in favour of visiting the museum.","keyword":"IDEA","secondBefore":"Joan thought it would be ","secondAfter":" to the museum.","answers":["a good idea to go"],"display":"a good idea to go","explanation":"a good idea to go. 'To be in favour of something' is paraphrased as 'to think that something would be a good idea', then changed accordingly to grammatically fit the context.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"Arthur has the talent to become a concert pianist.","keyword":"THAT","secondBefore":"Arthur is so ","secondAfter":" could become a concert pianist.","answers":["talented that he"],"display":"talented that he","explanation":"talented that he. 'So talented that' means 'talented enough to'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"'Do you know when the match starts, Sally?' asked Mary.","keyword":"IF","secondBefore":"Mary asked Sally ","secondAfter":" time the match started.","answers":["if he knew what/the","if she knew what/the"],"display":"if he knew what/the","explanation":"if he/she knew what/the. We don't really know if Sally is a boy or a girl, so both pronouns are acceptable. In the second part, the determiner 'what' and the definite article 'the' are interchangeable as the meaning remains unchanged.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"I knocked for ages at Ruth's door but I got no reply.","keyword":"LONG","secondBefore":"I ","secondAfter":" knocking at Ruth's door but I got no reply.","answers":["spent a long time","took a long time"],"display":"spent a long time","explanation":"spent/took a long time. Both verbs are fine, as they collocate well with the verb in Continuous tense.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"Everyone says that the band is planning to go on a world tour next year.","keyword":"SAID","secondBefore":"The band ","secondAfter":" planning to go on a world tour next year.","answers":["are said to be","is said to be"],"display":"are said to be","explanation":"are/is said to be. Both plural and singular of the auxiliary verb 'to be' can be used, as we can either think of it as a single group or as a number of individuals.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"I'd prefer not to cancel the meeting.","keyword":"CALL","secondBefore":"I'd rather ","secondAfter":" the meeting","answers":["not call off"],"display":"not call off","explanation":"not call off. I'd rather not means 'I would prefer not to'.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-21","examNumber":21,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 11","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"21 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 11","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Teenagers really do need more sleep","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["It has long been suspected that lack of sleep can actually 0 _____ to illness, particularly in young people. Research ",{"n":1,"options":["enclosing","combining","associating","involving"],"answer":"involving","answers":["involving"],"explanation":"D - involving. Taking into account, making them a part of it. 'Enclosing' has a more literal meaning of surrounding something or somebody. 'Combining' would mean putting them together.","skill":"Part 1"}," students aged 14-19 over a three-week period now appears to ",{"n":2,"options":["assure","confirm","defend","justify"],"answer":"confirm","answers":["confirm"],"explanation":"B - confirm. To confirm here is to once again prove that something is true. 'Assure' is mostly used with people. 'Justify' is to prove that something is necessary to do.","skill":"Part 1"}," this. The teenagers wore devices that recorded the movements they made, without being ",{"n":3,"options":["awake","aware","familiar","sensitive"],"answer":"aware","answers":["aware"],"explanation":"B - aware. The only adjective that collocates with 'of' preposition. Please keep in mind that very often the surrounding context words help you a great deal with choosing the right option.","skill":"Part 1"}," of them, that indicated they were asleep. The results were then ",{"n":4,"options":["compared","measured","balanced","qualified"],"answer":"compared","answers":["compared"],"explanation":"A - compared. This is another case where a preposition helps us choose the right option. Contextually, 'balanced to' could work equally well.","skill":"Part 1"}," to the number of illnesses that they had ",{"n":5,"options":["caught","affected","suffered","experienced"],"answer":"suffered","answers":["suffered"],"explanation":"C - suffered. 'Caught' and 'experienced' wouldn't need a preposition. 'Affected' is used with 'by'.","skill":"Part 1"}," from during the three weeks, in addition to the number of occasions on which they had been ",{"n":6,"options":["outside","remote","distant","absent"],"answer":"absent","answers":["absent"],"explanation":"D - absent. 'Outside of' would have worked here. The other two options do not collocate with 'from' at all.","skill":"Part 1"}," from school. What the study showed was that students who slept fewer than seven hours a night caught colds, flu and other relatively ",{"n":7,"options":["light","minor","smaller","slight"],"answer":"minor","answers":["minor"],"explanation":"B - minor. This is a tough one. It is important to know what collocates well with the word 'illness' to make the right call.","skill":"Part 1"}," illnesses more often. The problem is that as children enter their teens their natural sleeping patterns change, ",{"n":8,"options":["resulting","producing","finishing","forcing"],"answer":"resulting","answers":["resulting"],"explanation":"A - resulting. One more time where 'in' dictates the right choice.","skill":"Part 1"}," in them going to sleep later and therefore wanting to wake up later - but they still have to get up in the morning to go to school."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Music really can reduce that pain","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["A survey has recently been carried 0 _____ into the way music affects people in pain, and ",{"n":9,"answer":"what","answers":["what","something"],"explanation":"what/something. 'What/something ... is ...'. The verb 'to be' further in the sentence helps us make the right choice.","skill":"Part 2"}," it seems to show is that certain songs can actually reduce the sensation of physical pain. About 40% of people suffering ",{"n":10,"answer":"from","answers":["from"],"explanation":"from. Another collocation here. Interestingly, the same one is used in #5 in the previous part!","skill":"Part 2"}," continuous pain said that music helped them feel better, with an even higher figure ",{"n":11,"answer":"among","answers":["among","amongst","for"],"explanation":"among/amongst/for. Any of the given options have the meaning of happening in a particular group of people.","skill":"Part 2"}," young people: a remarkable two-thirds of those taking part ",{"n":12,"answer":"are","answers":["are"],"explanation":"are. 'Are reported to have said' is a present reporting structure referring to a past action.","skill":"Part 2"}," reported to have said it had had a positive effect ",{"n":13,"answer":"on","answers":["on"],"explanation":"on. To have an effect on something/somebody.","skill":"Part 2"}," their symptoms.  The type of music played appears to make less difference than might ",{"n":14,"answer":"be","answers":["be"],"explanation":"be. Another instance of passive voice, make sure to stick to Present tense.","skill":"Part 2"}," imagined. Pop music, as ",{"n":15,"answer":"long","answers":["long"],"explanation":"long. 'As long as' has the meaning 'if/provided that'. In other words, it introduces a condition that has to be met.","skill":"Part 2"}," as it is fairly gentle and not too loud, is slightly ahead of classical as the favourite for dealing with pain. Researchers believe that listening to your favourite music, ",{"n":16,"answer":"which","answers":["which"],"explanation":"which. This is a case of so-called non-restrictive clause. Basically, it is a choice between \"which\" and \"that\". If you struggle to see the difference between the two, follow punctuation marks - 'that' won't normally have a comma before it.","skill":"Part 2"}," may directly affect both your emotions and your thoughts, can have the very welcome effect of distracting you from what is hurting."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Letting the sea in","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["The small 0 _____ town of Medmerry has found an unusual way to reduce the risk of flooding: let the sea in. The scheme involved the ",{"n":17,"answer":"destruction","answers":["destruction"],"explanation":"destruction. A noun is needed here. The 'of the' structure helps us to find the right part of speech.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DESTROY"}," of part of the existing sea wall and the building of seven kilometres of new, higher ",{"n":18,"answer":"defences","answers":["defences","defenses"],"explanation":"defences/defenses. Both AmE/BrE spelling are accepted in Cambridge exams. The only difficulty here is to understand what part of speech \"inland\" is and what it means. It is an adjective and here it means 'further into the land and away from the sea'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DEFEND"}," further inland, closer to local communities. This has led to the ",{"n":19,"answer":"creation","answers":["creation"],"explanation":"creation. Another \"of\" structure with a noun.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CREATE"}," of a large area of wetland, capable of absorbing the energy of the waves and therefore bringing about a ",{"n":20,"answer":"substantial","answers":["substantial"],"explanation":"substantial. Don't be tempted to put a noun here because of the indefinite article before the gap - it refers to the noun after the gap.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SUBSTANCE"}," reduction in flooding at times of storm and high tides. According to environmental scientists, this ",{"n":21,"answer":"ambitious","answers":["ambitious"],"explanation":"ambitious. Full of ambition, grand and optimistic, this word can sometimes be used ironically.","skill":"Part 3","base":"AMBITION"}," project will also turn the whole area into a ",{"n":22,"answer":"massive","answers":["massive"],"explanation":"massive. Make sure to keep double S in this word.","skill":"Part 3","base":"MASS"}," nature reserve. It is already attracting large numbers of birds and other wildlife and eventually it should provide a safe home for some of the country's most ",{"n":23,"answer":"endangered","answers":["endangered"],"explanation":"endangered. \"Dangerous\" wouldn't work here because of the context - it wouldn't make sense to provide safe environment for such animals!","skill":"Part 3","base":"DANGER"}," species. This is bound to attract more visitors to the area, although it remains ",{"n":24,"answer":"unclear","answers":["unclear"],"explanation":"unclear. \"Although\" introduces contrast in the sentence and helps us use the negative prefix \"un-\".","skill":"Part 3","base":"CLEAR"},"whether numbers will have to be limited in order to protect the reserve."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"'Would you like to come to my party, Karen?', the girl said.","keyword":"INVITED","secondBefore":"The girl ","secondAfter":" party.","answers":["invited Karen to her"],"display":"invited Karen to her","explanation":"invited Karen to her. The key here is to use \"her\" instead of \"the\" because the girl says 'my party'. Using the definite article instead means you get only one of the two points.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"Two foolish people took no notice of the warning sign.","keyword":"PAID","secondBefore":"Two foolish people ","secondAfter":" the warning sign.","answers":["paid no attention to"],"display":"paid no attention to","explanation":"paid no attention to. Here we can't use \"didn't\" because \"paid\" is given in the past, so 'no' has to be used to express the negative meaning.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"The rescue workers had too little food to feed everyone.","keyword":"NOT","secondBefore":"There ","secondAfter":" the rescue workers to feed everyone.","answers":["was not enough food for","was not sufficient food for"],"display":"was not enough food for","explanation":"was not enough/sufficient food for. The only pitfall here is to make sure to remember that \"food\" is used as a singular noun, not plural. Uncountable nouns in English are grammatically treated as singular.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"Jack gets no exercise apart from walking to his car.","keyword":"ONLY","secondBefore":"The ","secondAfter":" walking to his car.","answers":["only exercise Jack gets is"],"display":"only exercise Jack gets is","explanation":"only exercise Jack gets is. Make sure to stick to Present Simple here, just like in the original sentence. The general rule of FCE Part 4 is to make as few changes to the original sentence as possible.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"Sonia wanted to be a nurse so she left her job as a teacher.","keyword":"GAVE","secondBefore":"Sonia ","secondAfter":" become a nurse.","answers":["gave up teaching to"],"display":"gave up teaching to","explanation":"gave up teaching to. 'To' expresses the idea of purpose. 'To give up' means to stop doing something.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"There's far less noise in this street than there used to be.","keyword":"NEARLY","secondBefore":"In this street, there isn't ","secondAfter":" there used to be.","answers":["nearly as much noise as","nearly so much noise as"],"display":"nearly as much noise as","explanation":"nearly as/so much noise as. 'Far less' expresses a strong comparison, so we must use 'nearly as much noise as' to preserve the comparative structure","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-22","examNumber":22,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 12","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"22 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 12","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Mars on Earth","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["The centre of Antarctica, where winter temperatures frequently fall to 0 _____ 80 degrees centigrade and for four months each year there is ",{"n":1,"options":["regular","constant","repeated","maintained"],"answer":"constant","answers":["constant"],"explanation":"B - constant. If something is constant, it is there all the time and doesn't change. Other words do not collocate with this word in the given context.","skill":"Part 1"}," darkness 24 hours a day, is one of the most extreme environments on Earth. It is also by ",{"n":2,"options":["far","much","large","long"],"answer":"far","answers":["far"],"explanation":"A - far. 'By far' here means 'to a much greater degree than anything else'.","skill":"Part 1"}," the most similar place to the surface of Mars, which is why a ",{"n":3,"options":["crew","cast","gang","team"],"answer":"team","answers":["team"],"explanation":"D - team. Crew is people operating some vehicle, such as a ship or a plane. A gang is a group of criminals. Cast refers to actors taking part in a play or a film.","skill":"Part 1"}," of thirteen scientists from the European Space Agency have spent nine months at a base there. They have been studying the physical and psychological ",{"n":4,"options":["results","effects","issues","risks"],"answer":"effects","answers":["effects"],"explanation":"B - effects. 'Issues' and 'risks' have a negative meaning, which is not implied here. 'Results' would require the preposition 'of' rather than 'on'.","skill":"Part 1"}," on humans of living in extreme conditions, in order to obtain a better understanding of the difficulties humans will ",{"n":5,"options":["oppose","dare","challenge","face"],"answer":"face","answers":["face"],"explanation":"D - face. This is the most common verb to use with something difficult or unpleasant. 'To face a problem/challenge/ enemy'.","skill":"Part 1"}," during the long flight to Mars and their stay there. Of course, space is ",{"n":6,"options":["unlike","contrary","unfamiliar","different"],"answer":"unlike","answers":["unlike"],"explanation":"A - unlike. 'Unlike' is followed directly by a noun. 'Different' would require 'from', and 'contrary' would require 'to' .","skill":"Part 1"}," Antarctica in that astronauts have to adapt to weightlessness, but the base is 3,200 metres above sea ",{"n":7,"options":["height","level","depth","scale"],"answer":"level","answers":["level"],"explanation":"B - level. 'Sea level' is a common phrase that means the height of an object relative to the sea.","skill":"Part 1"}," making it difficult to breathe there - as it can be on space flights. They have also, like space travellers, experienced strong feelings of being ",{"n":8,"options":["broken","cut","kept","taken"],"answer":"cut","answers":["cut"],"explanation":"B - cut. 'Cut off' means isolated, kept apart from something or somebody.","skill":"Part 1"}," off from civilisation."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Marathon in the mountains","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["The Ultra-Trail of Mont Blanc is an extreme marathon held annually 0 _____ 2003 in the mountains of France, Italy and Switzerland. Widely regarded ",{"n":9,"answer":"as","answers":["as"],"explanation":"as. 'Regarded as' means it is seen as something (in this case, seen as the most challenging race).","skill":"Part 2"}," one of the toughest races in the world, the Ultra-Trail takes place in August, ",{"n":10,"answer":"when","answers":["when"],"explanation":"when. It refers to the month of August, the choice here is fairly simple.","skill":"Part 2"}," temperatures frequently range from over 30°C at the lowest points of the course, to well ",{"n":11,"answer":"below","answers":["below","under"],"explanation":"below/under. We are talking about temperature here and in the previous one. Don't be confused by lowest and highest points of the course - these refer to the low and high altitudes. It is always much colder when you are high in the mountains.","skill":"Part 2"}," freezing at the highest. An Olympic marathon is 42.5 kilometres long, but ",{"n":12,"answer":"this","answers":["this"],"explanation":"this. 'But' introduces a contrast between a regular marathon and this one.","skill":"Part 2"}," one is over 160 kilometres in length and includes nine mountain peaks. That involves climbing a total of approximately 9,500 metres, ",{"n":13,"answer":"which","answers":["which"],"explanation":"which. A non-restrictive clause is used here. It adds non-essential information that can be skipped without ruining the sentence.","skill":"Part 2"}," is considerably higher than Everest. In ",{"n":14,"answer":"spite","answers":["spite"],"explanation":"spite. 'In spite of' shows contrast - even though the race is very challenging, it attracts a lot of athletes.","skill":"Part 2"}," of, or perhaps because of this, the annual event has become increasingly popular with runners, to ",{"n":15,"answer":"such","answers":["such"],"explanation":"such. 'To such an extent' - to the point that, so much that.","skill":"Part 2"}," an extent that the numbers have had to be limited to 2300. Only about half of these, ",{"n":16,"answer":"on","answers":["on"],"explanation":"on. 'On average' means roughly or approximately.","skill":"Part 2"}," average, are likely to finish the course, in times that typically vary from 20 hours to over 45. "]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Jobs in the future","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["In twenty years' time, which jobs will people still be doing, and which is it 0 _____ to assume will have gone forever? Today's young people should give that question careful ",{"n":17,"answer":"consideration","answers":["consideration"],"explanation":"consideration. We use an adjective + noun combination here. Do not use 'considering' - if there is a 'real' noun instead of the gerund (basically, when you take a verb and add -ing to make a noun) then you should go with it.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CONSIDER"}," before choosing a career. Clearly there will continue to be a need for staff in ",{"n":18,"answer":"existing","answers":["existing"],"explanation":"existing. Adjective is used here. Do not use 'existential' it refers to the idea of existence, i.e. meaning of life.","skill":"Part 3","base":"EXIST"}," professions such as medicine that involve caring for others, and also for people able to persuade, like ",{"n":19,"answer":"politicians","answers":["politicians"],"explanation":"politicians. Clearly we are looking for a profession here. An important thing is to use the plural form - because 'lawyers' are plural and connected to the gap with 'and'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"POLITICS"}," and lawyers. There will of course be no ",{"n":20,"answer":"shortage","answers":["shortage"],"explanation":"shortage. Shortage is lack of something, when something is not present in needed amount or quantity.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SHORT"}," of jobs in science and technology, particularly for individuals able to ",{"n":21,"answer":"specialise","answers":["specialise","specialize"],"explanation":"specialise/specialize. Both American and British spelling of this verb can be used to get the point for this answer.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SPECIAL"}," in newer fields such as biotechnology and microbiology. On the other hand, the already rapid ",{"n":22,"answer":"loss","answers":["loss"],"explanation":"loss. 'Rapid loss of jobs' requires a noun after 'rapid'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"LOSE"}," of jobs to robots will speed up even further, as they replace workers not only in manufacturing but also in the ",{"n":23,"answer":"construction","answers":["construction"],"explanation":"construction. Even though the previously used 'manufacturing' is formed with '-ing', if we mention the industry, it is 'construction'. 'Constructing' means the process of building something, not the business, industry or activity.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CONSTRUCT"}," industry. And as robots learn how to stack shelves, take over at check-outs or send us our online shopping, it won't be long before most supermarket jobs have ",{"n":24,"answer":"disappeared","answers":["disappeared"],"explanation":"disappeared. Contextually, we need a negative meaning, so we use the prefix dis-.","skill":"Part 3","base":"APPEAR"}," , too."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Amanda woke up late because she'd forgotten to set her alarm.","keyword":"HAVE","secondBefore":"If Amanda hadn't forgotten to set her alarm she ","secondAfter":" late.","answers":["wouldn't not have woken up","would not have woken up"],"display":"wouldn't not have woken up","explanation":"wouldn't/would not have woken up. Third conditional sentence is used here. Conditionals are common in Part 4, so make sure to revise zero, first, second and third conditionals.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"This Internet browser works far better than the others.","keyword":"NEARLY","secondBefore":"The other Internet browsers don't ","secondAfter":" this one.","answers":["work nearly as well as"],"display":"work nearly as well as","explanation":"work nearly as well as. Nearly is often used in negative phrases, in this case the structure is 'not nearly as + adjective/adverb + as'. Keep subject-verb pair in check - the verb has to be plural because we use 'browsers'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"'Can I borrow your phone for a minute, Jack?' asked Emma.","keyword":"WHETHER","secondBefore":"Emma asked Jack ","secondAfter":" phone.","answers":["whether she could borrow his"],"display":"whether she could borrow his","explanation":"whether she could borrow his. In reported speech, we have to take the so-called 'one step back' with the tenses. The original sentence is in Present Simple, so the reported one has to be in Past Simple.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"Raymond has so much skill as an artist that his drawings look like photographs.","keyword":"SUCH","secondBefore":"Raymond is ","secondAfter":" artist that his drawings look like photographs.","answers":["such a skillful","such a skilled"],"display":"such a skillful","explanation":"such a skillful/skilled. A straightforward transformation with some options. Both skillful and skilled can be used. Some people might choose to spell it 'skilful' with one l.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"I wish I hadn't stayed up late last night.","keyword":"BED","secondBefore":"I should ","secondAfter":" last night.","answers":["have gone to bed earlier"],"display":"have gone to bed earlier","explanation":"have gone to bed earlier. We express regret here in a slightly different way, nothing too complex though. After 'should', we use 'have + past participle' to show regret about the past.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"Our departure was delayed because another flight arrived late.","keyword":"CAUSED","secondBefore":"The delay to our departure ","secondAfter":" arrival of another flight.","answers":["was caused by the late"],"display":"was caused by the late","explanation":"was caused by the late. Passive voice is used to show that something was done to us or our situation. 'By' here introduces the cause of the situation, i.e. late arrival.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-23","examNumber":23,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 13","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"23 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 13","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Slacklining","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["Slacklining is an increasingly popular activity in London's parks. All you need if you want to 0 _____ a go at slacklining is two trees about fifteen metres apart, a five-centimetre-wide strap of around the same length and the equipment to ",{"n":1,"options":["join","attach","unite","stick"],"answer":"attach","answers":["attach"],"explanation":"B - attach. The only other verb that goes with 'to' is D - stick but it can't be used here. Sticking means connecting by gluing, which is not what the context means.","skill":"Part 1"}," the strap to the trees. The slackline is not a rope, but a flat piece of strong webbing material. The ",{"n":2,"options":["aim","target","plan","schedule"],"answer":"aim","answers":["aim"],"explanation":"A - aim. The general idea or purpose is described here. 'Plan' means something more grand and elaborate. 'Target' means a particular point or thing, which is not mentioned. 'Schedule' refers to a certain time and does not fit the context at all.","skill":"Part 1"}," is to try to walk along the line without ",{"n":3,"options":["dropping","missing","losing","slipping"],"answer":"losing","answers":["losing"],"explanation":"C - losing. 'Lose' is the only verb of all the options that collocates well with 'balance'.","skill":"Part 1"}," your balance. As the line is usually ",{"n":4,"options":["positioned","stood","settled","arranged"],"answer":"positioned","answers":["positioned"],"explanation":"A - positioned. To position is to specifically put something in a particular place. Answers 'B' and 'C' do not fit the context at all. 'Arrange' implies multiple objects or a more complex system.","skill":"Part 1"}," about a metre or so above the ground, there is no ",{"n":5,"options":["deep","great","wide","tall"],"answer":"great","answers":["great"],"explanation":"B - great. The only adjective in the list that goes well with 'risk'.","skill":"Part 1"}," risk of injury if you don't ",{"n":6,"options":["realise","achieve","manage","succeed"],"answer":"manage","answers":["manage"],"explanation":"C - manage. To do something successfully. 'Succeed' doesn't fit, as you would need a gerund after it: 'Succeed in doing something'.","skill":"Part 1"}," to stay on the line. Other street sports, such as skateboarding and parkour are much more dangerous. Basically, slacklining ",{"n":7,"options":["asks","requests","calls","requires"],"answer":"requires","answers":["requires"],"explanation":"D - requires. Needs, demands practice - other examples of verbs that work with the word 'practice'.","skill":"Part 1"}," quite a lot of practice, but it is something you can learn to do. At first, most people like to ",{"n":8,"options":["keep","hold","touch","stay"],"answer":"hold","answers":["hold"],"explanation":"B - hold. 'Hold on to something/someone' is to maintain physical contact with it for balance or reassurance.","skill":"Part 1"}," on to a friend's hand until get used to the feeling of the moving line under their feet."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Blanca Huertas: butterfly expert","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["As a little girl growing 0 _____ in Colombia, Blanca Huertas was fascinated by wildlife, and especially by butterflies. In those days you couldn't buy butterfly nets locally, so her father made ",{"n":9,"answer":"one","answers":["one"],"explanation":"one. 'One' is used to avoid word repetition - in this case, the author meant a butterfly net.","skill":"Part 2"}," for her using the end of a broom. He also bought her some books and took her to different places at the weekends so ",{"n":10,"answer":"that","answers":["that"],"explanation":"that. 'So that' has the meaning 'to/in order to'. The purpose of some action is shown.","skill":"Part 2"}," she could see different species and learn about different types of habitat. It was at this time that she started her butterfly collection. ",{"n":11,"answer":"when","answers":["when"],"explanation":"when. A particular moment in the past is referred to here.","skill":"Part 2"}," she was sixteen, Blanca went to university to study biology. Her professor convinced her that the study of butterflies ",{"n":12,"answer":"could","answers":["could"],"explanation":"could. A possibility is introduced here. Note that it is 'could' and not 'can' because throughout the sentence the Past Simple is used.","skill":"Part 2"}," become a career, not just a hobby. What's more, he encouraged Blanca to apply ",{"n":13,"answer":"for","answers":["for"],"explanation":"for. Apply for something - to formally and documentarily announce your desire to do or have something.","skill":"Part 2"}," a place on a field trip to the Cauca region of the Amazon, ",{"n":14,"answer":"which","answers":["which"],"explanation":"which. As with most Part 2 tasks, we can see the usage of a non-restrictive clause. Since the comma is present, we cannot use 'that' here.","skill":"Part 2"}," was being organised by students from Oxford and Cambridge.  The two-month trip was an incredible experience. Blanca rediscovered a butterfly that hadn't ",{"n":15,"answer":"been","answers":["been"],"explanation":"been. Past Perfect Passive — ('had been seen') - required because the rediscovery happened after a long period during which the butterfly had not been seen.","skill":"Part 2"}," seen for thirty years and collected information about new species. ",{"n":16,"answer":"as","answers":["as"],"explanation":"as. As a result of something. The required meaning is easier to find when you take a look at the second part of the sentence.","skill":"Part 2"}," a result of the trip, the region was turned into a national park."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · In praise of plastic","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Wherever you are, you're likely to be surrounded by objects made of some kind of plastic material. There are many types of plastic, each one 0 _____ for a particular use. Thin, flexible plastic is good for making objects such as bags, whereas thick, hard plastic is a ",{"n":17,"answer":"useful","answers":["useful"],"explanation":"useful. Various applications of plastic are described here and how each could be fit for a particular purpose.","skill":"Part 3","base":"USE"}," material for making functional objects, and has been used to make very ",{"n":18,"answer":"attractive","answers":["attractive"],"explanation":"attractive. An adjective with a positive meaning is suggested by the context of this sentence.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ATTRACT"}," pieces of furniture. Although plastic has a ",{"n":19,"answer":"reputation","answers":["reputation"],"explanation":"reputation. Plastic is known for its certain qualities - it has a reputation of being 'cheap and disposable'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"REPUTE"}," for being cheap and disposable, in the hands of a good ",{"n":20,"answer":"designer","answers":["designer"],"explanation":"designer. Judging by the indefinite article it should be clear that we need a profession here.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DESIGN"}," it can be used to make objects of great beauty. As well as offering ",{"n":21,"answer":"extremely","answers":["extremely"],"explanation":"extremely. An adverb to further intensify the adjective 'high'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"EXTREME"}," high quality at a low price, plastic can take almost any shape, colour or consistency. Plastic can also be seen as an environmentally friendly ",{"n":22,"answer":"choice","answers":["choice"],"explanation":"choice. Make sure to choose the right spelling - sometimes students mistakenly write this word with an 's' instead of 'c'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CHOOSE"}," of material. It can be used in place of wood and other materials that are in short supply in their ",{"n":23,"answer":"natural","answers":["natural"],"explanation":"natural. A straightforward noun-to-adjective transformation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"NATURE"}," state, and it is also very hard-wearing. A good plastic chair, for example, could last you a lifetime and when you no longer wanted it, then it would probably be possible to ",{"n":24,"answer":"recycle","answers":["recycle"],"explanation":"recycle. To use something again, to give in a second life, to repurpose it.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CYCLE"}," the material"]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Heidi stayed with a friend when she went to London.","keyword":"PUT","secondBefore":"A friend ","secondAfter":" when she went to London.","answers":["put Heidi up"],"display":"put Heidi up","explanation":"put Heidi up. 'To put somebody up' / 'to put up somewhere' means to give somebody a place to stay for the night or to spend the night there, respectively.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"The singer dislikes people asking for his autograph in the street.","keyword":"LIKE","secondBefore":"The singer ","secondAfter":" people ask him for his autograph in the street.","answers":["does not like it when","does not like it if"],"display":"does not like it when","explanation":"does not like it when/if. An important point here is that we need an object. 'It' serves as an object in this sentence.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"I advised Kylie to enrol at a gym which she did.","keyword":"ADVICE","secondBefore":"Kylie ","secondAfter":" and enrolled at a gym.","answers":["took my advice"],"display":"took my advice","explanation":"took my advice. It is worth noting that in the original sentence 'advise' is a verb and the transformed sentence uses the noun form. Note the difference in spelling.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"I had no idea that a famous film star was born in the town.","keyword":"UNAWARE","secondBefore":"I ","secondAfter":" fact that a famous film star was born in the town.","answers":["was (completely) unaware of the"],"display":"was (completely) unaware of the","explanation":"was (completely) unaware of the. 'of the' structure is the most challenging part of this otherwise simple transformation. 'Completely' is optional and does not affect your score.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"Simon was the only student to apply for a scholarship.","keyword":"NONE","secondBefore":"Apart ","secondAfter":" the students applied for a scholarship.","answers":["from Simon none of"],"display":"from Simon none of","explanation":"from Simon none of. 'None of' means not a single one, nobody. 'Apart from' means 'with the exception of, other than'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"Debbie wore a jumper because she thought it might be cold in the mountains.","keyword":"CASE","secondBefore":"Debbie wore a jumper ","secondAfter":" in the mountains.","answers":["in case it was cold","in case it got cold"],"display":"in case it was cold","explanation":"in case it was/got cold. One possible mistake here is to use Present Simple. Remember to stick to the same tense throughout the sentence or clause.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-24","examNumber":24,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 14","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"24 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 14","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Vera Neumann: fabric designer","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["Vera Neumann was a designer and businesswoman whose products 0 _____ their way into the homes of people across the USA. Vera was born in Connecticut in 1907 and showed artistic ",{"n":1,"options":["training","talent","expert","gift"],"answer":"talent","answers":["talent"],"explanation":"B - talent. This goes well with the adjective 'artistic'. 'Training' means some time spent learning something. 'Expert' is not a quality, the word would be 'expertise'.","skill":"Part 1"}," from an early age. After attending art college in New York, she got a job as a textile designer, but didn't like being ",{"n":2,"options":["led","directed","told","forced"],"answer":"told","answers":["told"],"explanation":"C - told. We have a case of the passive voice and this affects our choice. The part 'what to do' only works with 'told' here.","skill":"Part 1"}," what to do. Determined to develop her own styles, Vera started to produce tablecloths, each item printed by hand in her kitchen. But it was her scarves that ",{"n":3,"options":["got","did","gained","made"],"answer":"made","answers":["made"],"explanation":"D - made. 'To make a name' means to become famous, recognized or well-known.","skill":"Part 1"}," Vera's name. Good fabric was in short ",{"n":4,"options":["supply","availability","quantity","delivery"],"answer":"supply","answers":["supply"],"explanation":"A - supply. If something is in short supply, there is not enough of it. 'Low quantity' would work as well, but it sounds more technical.","skill":"Part 1"}," during the Second World War, but Vera was lucky enough to ",{"n":5,"options":["fall","come","happen","run"],"answer":"come","answers":["come"],"explanation":"B - come. 'To come across something' means to find it by chance, accidentally, without meaning to.","skill":"Part 1"}," across some silk left over from the manufacture of parachutes. Vera used it to design scarves with floral, abstract and geometric designs. These were an ",{"n":6,"options":["acute","urgent","instant","extreme"],"answer":"instant","answers":["instant"],"explanation":"C - instant. An adjective that collocates well with 'success'. 'Instant success' is a fixed collocation meaning success achieved immediately.","skill":"Part 1"}," success when they appeared in department stores and during the 1950s they were the ",{"n":7,"options":["height","peak","top","crown"],"answer":"height","answers":["height"],"explanation":"A - height. A fixed expression meaning extremely fashionable, especially at a particular time.","skill":"Part 1"}," of fashion, being worn by celebrities such as the film star Marilyn Monroe. By 1960, the company which Vera had ",{"n":8,"options":["worked out","put on","carried off","set up"],"answer":"set up","answers":["set up"],"explanation":"D - set up. 'Set up' in this context means 'found, establish'.","skill":"Part 1"}," was employing 200 staff and producing 130 patterns per season."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Tree-climbing","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["Many people will look back on tree-climbing 0 _____ a simple childhood pleasure and a natural test of human strength, stamina and agility. But these days, it has turned ",{"n":9,"answer":"into","answers":["into"],"explanation":"into. 'Turn into' means to transform into something, to become something else. Don't be tempted to answer 'turn in' as it means to return something, i.e. 'police officers have to turn in their firearms at the end of their duty'.","skill":"Part 2"}," a more serious activity and is ",{"n":10,"answer":"one","answers":["one"],"explanation":"one. '... of the' helps us make the right choice here. One of many, not the only one.","skill":"Part 2"}," of the fastest-growing adventure sports in the country. ",{"n":11,"answer":"before","answers":["before"],"explanation":"before. The context helps to understand how things were previously, when gyms hadn't become more popular and widespread.","skill":"Part 2"}," the invention of expensive gyms with indoor climbing walls, tree-climbing was a cheap and simple way to get a similar type of exercise. These days, if you want to ",{"n":12,"answer":"take","answers":["take"],"explanation":"take. To participate, to become a member or contestant.","skill":"Part 2"}," part in an organised tree-climbing activity, you ",{"n":13,"answer":"have","answers":["have","need"],"explanation":"have/need. Both modals that show obligation fit the context here. 'Should' can't be used as it means that it is a recommendation rather than a requirement, while the context suggests otherwise.","skill":"Part 2"}," to wear a helmet and attach yourself to ropes in ",{"n":14,"answer":"case","answers":["case"],"explanation":"case. 'In case' - in the event of something, if something (undesirable) happens.","skill":"Part 2"}," you should lose your footing. But otherwise, tree- climbing is a very eco-friendly sport, involving minimum damage to trees or the environment. Getting off the ground is perhaps the most challenging part of a climb. ",{"n":15,"answer":"after","answers":["after"],"explanation":"after. 'After that' refers to the stage following getting off the ground","skill":"Part 2"}," that, it's about using your legs, ",{"n":16,"answer":"which","answers":["which"],"explanation":"which. A case of non-restrictive relative clause can be seen here.","skill":"Part 2"}," are much stronger than your arms, to drive yourself up the tree. Reaching the top and then abseiling down again is a source of great satisfaction."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Games in space","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["For astronauts on long missions into space, 0 _____ can be a real problem. In order to help the astronauts, ",{"n":17,"answer":"scientists","answers":["scientists"],"explanation":"scientists. Astronauts and doctors are mentioned here, so it is only natural to choose another profession in plural form. Mind the spelling, a mistake will render the right answer useless.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SCIENCE"}," and doctors need to find out what this feels like. As part of the ",{"n":18,"answer":"research","answers":["research"],"explanation":"research. 'Research' here is a noun used before another noun (attributive noun).","skill":"Part 3","base":"SEARCH"}," programme, six volunteers will be locked away for 520 days to see what effect this has on their mind and body. During their time in ",{"n":19,"answer":"isolation","answers":["isolation"],"explanation":"isolation. An easy to choose noun. A reminder not to use 'isolating' - choosing the proper noun instead of a gerund form is always preferable.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ISOLATE"}," the volunteers will be able to communicate with their controllers, but only in a way that replicates the astronauts' experience. For example, the further away from 'Earth' they get, the longer the delay in the signal, so they will be ",{"n":20,"answer":"unable","answers":["unable"],"explanation":"unable. The word 'delay' suggests that real-time conversation is impossible, so we need to use a negative prefix.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ABLE"}," to have a conversation in real time. As part of the experiment, the volunteers will be allowed to play computer games. There will be a ",{"n":21,"answer":"mixture","answers":["mixture"],"explanation":"mixture. 'A mixture of' is the correct countable noun form. 'Mix' would not fit grammatically here. Another point to keep in mind is that you ALWAYS have to change the initial word, so 'mix' can't be used without any changes.","skill":"Part 3","base":"MIX"}," of solo games and competitive games available. Afterwards, they will be asked to give ",{"n":22,"answer":"feedback","answers":["feedback"],"explanation":"feedback. A noun is needed here. The only difficulty is that there is no article before the gap, which suggests either a plural or uncountable noun. 'Feedback' is uncountable.","skill":"Part 3","base":"FEED"}," on how hard they found the games to play and what their ",{"n":23,"answer":"emotional","answers":["emotional"],"explanation":"emotional. Easy choice of an adjective. Make sure to get the spelling right.","skill":"Part 3","base":"EMOTION"}," state was at the time. As a result of the ",{"n":24,"answer":"information","answers":["information"],"explanation":"information. Another straightforward transformation.","skill":"Part 3","base":"INFORM"}," gathered, it may be possible to create special software for crews on future missions."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Learning to sew seemed a waste of time to Lisa.","keyword":"POINT","secondBefore":"Lisa couldn't ","secondAfter":" learning to sew.","answers":["see any/the point in","see much/the point in"],"display":"see any/the point in","explanation":"see any/much/the point in. 'To see a point in something' means to consider it useful, worth knowing or doing.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"I reckon Barry was relieved to find his missing cellphone again.","keyword":"BEEN","secondBefore":"Barry must ","secondAfter":" he found his missing cellphone.","answers":["have been relieved when"],"display":"have been relieved when","explanation":"have been relieved when. 'Been' is a Past Participle form of 'to be' and therefore it suggests that a Perfect tense should be used. We use the Present Perfect for deduction about a past situation.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"According to the gossip, the actress will be getting married soon.","keyword":"RUMOURED","secondBefore":"The actress ","secondAfter":" getting married soon.","answers":["is rumored to be"],"display":"is rumored to be","explanation":"is rumored to be. Passive voice is used here. Note the usage of Present Continuous ('to be getting married') to show an arrangement planned for the future.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"Pamela said that she regretted sending that angry text message to Harry.","keyword":"ONLY","secondBefore":"'If ","secondAfter":" that angry text message to Harry', said Pamela.","answers":["only I had not sent","only I had hadn't sent"],"display":"only I had not sent","explanation":"only I had not/hadn't sent. Third Conditional is used here to show regret.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"Ashley always eats a big breakfast before going out in the morning.","keyword":"WITHOUT","secondBefore":"Ashley never goes out in the morning ","secondAfter":" a big breakfast.","answers":["without (first) eating (first) eaten","without (first) having (first) eaten"],"display":"without (first) eating (first) eaten","explanation":"without (first) eating/having (first) eaten. 'Without doing something'. The negative is stated at the beginning ('never').","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"Liam has decided not to go to football practice any more.","keyword":"GIVE","secondBefore":"Liam has decided ","secondAfter":" to football practice.","answers":["to give up going"],"display":"to give up going","explanation":"to give up going. 'Give up' phrasal verb is necessary to know to get the answer right.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-25","examNumber":25,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 15","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"25 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 15","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · The joy of photography","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["Photography is a hobby with wide 0 _____. And I don't mean taking photos on your mobile phone, though it cannot be ",{"n":1,"options":["rejected","contradicted","denied","refused"],"answer":"denied","answers":["denied"],"explanation":"C - denied. 'Cannot be denied' is a set phrase that means that nobody would argue against the expressed idea, e.g. 'it cannot be denied that higher education greatly improves the odds of getting a well-paid job for most people'.","skill":"Part 1"}," that such pictures can be surprisingly good these days. Serious photography means taking the ",{"n":2,"options":["trouble","care","effort","concern"],"answer":"trouble","answers":["trouble"],"explanation":"A - trouble. To take the trouble to do something means to make an effort to do it. Even though the word 'effort' describes the idea well, grammatically it cannot be used here - we can't say 'to take effort'. Other phrases with these words: 'to make an effort to do something', 'to take care of something', 'to concern oneself with something'.","skill":"Part 1"}," to do some research, exploring the technical ",{"n":3,"options":["characteristics","forms","qualities","aspects"],"answer":"aspects","answers":["aspects"],"explanation":"D - aspects. Aspects are technical parts of something, like a process or an activity.","skill":"Part 1"}," of the subject and investing in what might be quite expensive equipment. So why take up photography? Firstly, it allows you to ",{"n":4,"options":["seize","capture","grab","catch"],"answer":"capture","answers":["capture"],"explanation":"B - capture. 'To capture a picture' is a common collocation relating to photography.","skill":"Part 1"}," special moments that you want to remember forever. In addition, it ",{"n":5,"options":["motivates","renews","stimulates","reacts"],"answer":"stimulates","answers":["stimulates"],"explanation":"C - stimulates. If something stimulates you, it pushes or forces you to do it. 'Motivates' fits grammatically, but it doesn't fit the idea. You don't need to be motivated to make good pictures - it is a natural desire that requires no motivation. Moreover, 'stimulate imagination' is a much stronger and more natural collocation than 'motivate imagination'. The remaining two verbs do not fit the context.","skill":"Part 1"}," your imagination because you are always in search of ideas for original and out of the ordinary photos. However, a lot of practice is required before you ",{"n":6,"options":["manage","succeed","achieve","reach"],"answer":"manage","answers":["manage"],"explanation":"A - manage. The preposition 'to' limits our choice of the verb. Compare: 'to succeed in something', 'to achieve something', 'to reach something'.","skill":"Part 1"}," to take really good pictures on a regular basis. When you finally do it, it will be a ",{"n":7,"options":["reason","cause","source","means"],"answer":"source","answers":["source"],"explanation":"C - source. A source is a place, either literal or figurative, that has a lot of something. Can be used both positively and negatively, e.g. 'a source of many troubles'.","skill":"Part 1"}," of great satisfaction for you. Photography can also transform the way you look at the world. You start to see details that in the past you used to miss ",{"n":8,"options":["utterly","completely","fully","absolutely"],"answer":"completely","answers":["completely"],"explanation":"B - completely. If you miss something completely, you do not notice it at all. The frequency of collocations such as 'miss something utterly/completely' is much lower than 'miss something completely'.","skill":"Part 1"},". All in all, it's a highly absorbing hobby."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Running a marathon","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["So you want to run a marathon? There is 0 _____ doubt that running 42 kilometres is a great achievement. Many training plans involve running four times a week for at ",{"n":9,"answer":"least","answers":["least"],"explanation":"least. 'At least' expresses the idea 'at the minimum, no less than'.","skill":"Part 2"}," three months, and sometimes longer. Experts strongly recommend that you should ",{"n":10,"answer":"get","answers":["get"],"explanation":"get. 'Get used' means some change - exactly the idea that is expressed in the context. 'Be used' means that you already are in the habit - this contradicts the context as it is suggested that you should take it slow.","skill":"Part 2"}," used to running long distances gradually. If you don't, it can increase the chances ",{"n":11,"answer":"of","answers":["of"],"explanation":"of. To increase the chance of doing something. Choosing 'of' is dictated by the noun 'chance' here.","skill":"Part 2"}," picking up an injury. It ",{"n":12,"answer":"is","answers":["is"],"explanation":"is. A passive voice form is used here, it is a so-called impersonal structure, otherwise referred to as 'distancing'.","skill":"Part 2"}," generally thought that runners should initially go on fairly relaxed training runs. The pace should be gentle enough to allow you ",{"n":13,"answer":"to","answers":["to"],"explanation":"to. 'To allow somebody to do something'","skill":"Part 2"}," have a conversation with someone running alongside you.  Don't make the mistake of eating too little before the race, or you will rapidly run ",{"n":14,"answer":"out","answers":["out"],"explanation":"out. If you run out of something (e.g. energy, money, patience) it means you no longer have it because you have spent or wasted it all.","skill":"Part 2"}," of energy. But ",{"n":15,"answer":"Neither","answers":["Neither"],"explanation":"Neither. 'Neither' continues the negative idea from the previous sentence and triggers inversion ('Neither should you…').","skill":"Part 2"}," should you eat a large meal. It goes without saying that choosing the right footwear is also essential. ",{"n":16,"answer":"Whatever","answers":["Whatever"],"explanation":"Whatever. Here, the message is 'no matter what you do, do (or don't do) this.'.","skill":"Part 2"}," you do, avoid clothes made of cotton and go for artificial materials, or even some types of wool such as merino. Choose clothing that will be comfortable."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · An interesting new planet","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Until recently, the 0 _____ of planets outside our own solar system was difficult to prove. Now, thanks to increasingly ",{"n":17,"answer":"sensitive","answers":["sensitive"],"explanation":"sensitive. Here the word 'sensitive' means being able to pick up more detail and giving overall better information.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SENSE"}," equipment, hundreds have been discovered orbiting distant stars. Recent ",{"n":18,"answer":"investigations","answers":["investigations"],"explanation":"investigations. It is important to understand that only the plural form of the noun works here, as there is no article before 'increasingly'. With singular form, it would need to be 'A recent investigation'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"INVESTIGATE"}," of the nearest star to Earth, Proxima Centauri, have led to a ",{"n":19,"answer":"significant","answers":["significant"],"explanation":"significant. The only adjective that works here. Note that 'signifying' would usually be followed by an object, i.e. 'a discovery signifying that we are not alone in the universe'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SIGNIFY"}," discovery: a rocky planet similar in size to the Earth which may have liquid water on its surface. Although the new planet is ",{"n":20,"answer":"considerably","answers":["considerably"],"explanation":"considerably. We are looking for an adverb - because the gap is next to a comparative form of an adjective. Adverb + adjective is a common combination that you will see in FCE Use of English Part 3.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CONSIDER"}," closer to Proxima Centauri than the Earth is to the Sun, there is still a ",{"n":21,"answer":"possibility","answers":["possibility"],"explanation":"possibility. Possibility of life on the planet.","skill":"Part 3","base":"POSSIBLE"}," of life there. This is because the star is much smaller and cooler than our sun, so conditions on the planet may be ",{"n":22,"answer":"comfortable","answers":["comfortable"],"explanation":"comfortable. Another grammatically possible word is 'comforting', however it does not fit as the meaning is different. Comforting means giving you peace of mind, making you less worried or sad.","skill":"Part 3","base":"COMFORT"}," enough to support life. Temperatures on the planet will be ",{"n":23,"answer":"dependent","answers":["dependent"],"explanation":"dependent. 'Dependent' is the adjective form required here. Note the spelling: 'Dependant' (with an 'a' instead of 'e') is a noun (British spelling) meaning a person who relies on someone else.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DEPEND"}," on whether there is an atmosphere surrounding it. Travelling to Proxima Centauri and exploring its planet is totally ",{"n":24,"answer":"unreal","answers":["unreal"],"explanation":"unreal. Two reasons to use 'unreal' rather than 'real'. First of all, the context that follows the gap suggests impossibility of such expedition. Secondly, you ALWAYS have to change the word in this task, it never remains the same.","skill":"Part 3","base":"REAL"}," at the moment. Despite it being one of the nearest stars to the sun, it would take thousands of years to get there using current technology."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Going to bed early has a big effect on how Leila feels the next morning.","keyword":"DIFFERENCE","secondBefore":"If Leila goes to bed early, it ","secondAfter":" how she feels the next morning.","answers":["makes all the difference"],"display":"makes all the difference","explanation":"makes all the difference. 'To make all the difference' is to affect something greatly.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"Even though Max took the early bus, he was still late for work.","keyword":"FACT","secondBefore":"Max was still late for work in ","secondAfter":" he took the early bus.","answers":["spite of the fact"],"display":"spite of the fact","explanation":"spite of the fact. 'In spite of' is similar to 'despite', but we cannot use the latter here as we have 'in' before the gap.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"Last year I decided I wouldn't eat fast food for lunch any more.","keyword":"GIVE","secondBefore":"Last year I decided ","secondAfter":" fast food for lunch.","answers":["to give up (eating)"],"display":"to give up (eating)","explanation":"to give up (eating). In this context, you can both 'give up fast food' and 'give up eating fast food'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"Melanie goes jogging and answers her important emails before breakfast.","keyword":"WELL","secondBefore":"Melanie goes jogging ","secondAfter":" her important emails before breakfast.","answers":["as well as answers"],"display":"as well as answers","explanation":"as well as answers. Notably, 'answering' shouldn't be used because 'goes answering' means she literally walks somewhere to answer these emails. The key phrase here is 'as well as'. It is also worth noting that the sentence keeps two parallel present simple verbs: 'goes' and 'answers'. 'As well as' links the two actions.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"My television needs fixing as soon as possible.","keyword":"GET","secondBefore":"I must ","secondAfter":" as soon as possible","answers":["get my television fixed"],"display":"get my television fixed","explanation":"get my television fixed. 'To have something done' shows that somebody else is going to provide the service to you. It highlights that you are not doing it yourself.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"Clara says she doesn't usually go out at the weekend.","keyword":"UNUSUAL","secondBefore":"Clara says it ","secondAfter":" go out at the weekend.","answers":["is unusual for her to"],"display":"is unusual for her to","explanation":"is unusual for her to. A fairly straightforward transformation. It is unusual for somebody to do something.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-26","examNumber":26,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 16","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"26 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 16","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Off the beaten track","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["The expression 'getting off the beaten track' is used to 0 _____ to the experience of avoiding famous tourist attractions and choosing instead to explore less well-known places when travelling. For many people the whole ",{"n":1,"options":["worth","point","aim","profit"],"answer":"point","answers":["point"],"explanation":"B - point. If you talk about the point of something, you mean the purpose of the action or activity, why you are doing it to begin with. 'Aim' is about what you want to achieve - similar to 'purpose', but more specific. Compare: 'our aim is to get across the country on bicycles' and 'The point of going by bikes is to see whether we are physically fit'. 'Worth' and 'profit' do not fit this context.","skill":"Part 1"}," of travel is to visit cities such as Paris or Venice that have a great ",{"n":2,"options":["favour","approval","reputation","opinion"],"answer":"reputation","answers":["reputation"],"explanation":"C - reputation. To be well-known for something. 'Favour' and 'approval' would normally need an object, e.g. 'to win somebody's favour', 'to have somebody's approval to do something'.","skill":"Part 1"}," as places of beauty and historic importance. Furthermore, they are not particularly ",{"n":3,"options":["bothered","interrupted","offended","disturbed"],"answer":"bothered","answers":["bothered"],"explanation":"A - bothered. If you are not bothered by something, then you do not see it as a problem. This phrase has more figurative meaning in comparison with 'disturbed' or 'offended' - both suggest stronger emotional reaction rather than mild inconvenience. 'Interrupted' does not make much sense in this context.","skill":"Part 1"}," by the crowds that are usually found in such locations. But for others who have more of a ",{"n":4,"options":["feeling","impression","mood","sense"],"answer":"sense","answers":["sense"],"explanation":"D - sense. Yet another tricky collocation - 'a sense of adventure'. As a foreign speaker, you might struggle with collocations because more often than not, they are formed culturally over the years, and some of them might not sound very natural to you at first.","skill":"Part 1"}," of adventure, a good holiday must ",{"n":5,"options":["possess","consist","involve","concern"],"answer":"involve","answers":["involve"],"explanation":"C - involve. We are limited by the preposition following the gap. Compare: 'To consist OF something' and 'To involve something'. 'Possess' has the same meaning as 'have', but it is usually not used with inanimate objects. 'Concern' means 'to be about something', but it does not collocate well with the list of words after the gap.","skill":"Part 1"}," unfamiliar experiences, even taking some risks. Travelling off the beaten track may be done by some students who don't ",{"n":6,"options":["fix","stick","fasten","attach"],"answer":"stick","answers":["stick"],"explanation":"B - stick. All the words here convey the same idea, but only 'stick' works well in the more figurative meaning that is used here.","skill":"Part 1"}," to a rigid plan, but make decisions about what to do depending on how they feel. Other travellers prefer to spend money on guided tours to unusual locations. Such tours are designed to ",{"n":7,"options":["please","fit","agree","meet"],"answer":"meet","answers":["meet"],"explanation":"D - meet. 'Meet' is the only one that collocates well with 'needs'. This is a less obvious one. When preparing, it is always a good idea to use a collocations dictionary to check yourself. I highly recommend freecollocation.com.","skill":"Part 1"}," their particular needs, and all the arrangements are made for them. However people choose to get off the beaten track, the hope is always the same: to have a special, often unique ",{"n":8,"options":["experience","understanding","awareness","knowledge"],"answer":"experience","answers":["experience"],"explanation":"A - experience. Note the indefinite article used before the phrase. 'An experience' is a memorable event that you learn from. It can be both pleasant and unpleasant ones.","skill":"Part 1"}," of a different culture."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Keep on learning!","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["It doesn't matter 0 _____ you are still at school or in full-time employment, making the effort to learn new things is very important. Most of us have a few subjects on ",{"n":9,"answer":"which","answers":["which"],"explanation":"which. A pronoun that helps connect the ideas here. 'On that' is not used in a situation like this.","skill":"Part 2"}," we focus. These may be associated ",{"n":10,"answer":"with","answers":["with"],"explanation":"with. 'Associated with' means 'connected with' here.","skill":"Part 2"}," our study or job, or sometimes a hobby. ",{"n":11,"answer":"While","answers":["While","whereas"],"explanation":"While/whereas. In a sentence where 'while' is used for contrast, 'whereas' can be used to the same effect. 'While' is widely used and therefore is more natural, and 'whereas' is considered to be more formal.","skill":"Part 2"}," it is obviously important to develop a deep understanding of ",{"n":12,"answer":"what","answers":["what"],"explanation":"what. Remember that 'what' will not always mean a question and the associated change in word order that comes with it (verb before the subject).","skill":"Part 2"}," matters to us most, it is equally worthwhile to extend our range of knowledge beyond what we are familiar with, and that is true at ",{"n":13,"answer":"any","answers":["any"],"explanation":"any. 'At any age', but 'at all ages'. Since 'age' is singular here, we go with the first option.","skill":"Part 2"}," age.  So the best advice is to find the time to ",{"n":14,"answer":"Take","answers":["Take"],"explanation":"Take. The most difficult gap here, it expects you to know the phrasal verb 'to take on something', which means 'to accept some responsibility or duty'. To take on a new challenge means to face or accept it.","skill":"Part 2"}," on new challenges and learn new skills outside the areas where we feel most comfortable. People often choose subjects ",{"n":15,"answer":"Such","answers":["Such"],"explanation":"Such. 'Such as' is the only way in this context to introduce a list. We never say 'like as', we can only use 'like'.","skill":"Part 2"}," as new languages, computer skills, or painting. If you can't get to a class, then you can go online. Online courses can easily ",{"n":16,"answer":"Be","answers":["Be"],"explanation":"Be. An easy case of passive voice that should not pose any problems to a B2-C1 student.","skill":"Part 2"}," found, and learning online means you put in as much time as you want each day."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · The first coffee shops","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["There is some 0 _____ evidence to suggest that coffee was cultivated in Africa in the tenth century, but drinking coffee didn't become ",{"n":17,"answer":"fashionable","answers":["fashionable"],"explanation":"fashionable. An adjective with the meaning 'popular, trendy, in demand'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"FASHION"}," in Europe until the mid-seventeenth century, with many visitors to London claiming that going to a coffee shop was one of the great ",{"n":18,"answer":"pleasures","answers":["pleasures"],"explanation":"pleasures. Note the plural form - it absolutely has to be used here because of 'one of the' structures, which suggests plurality.","skill":"Part 3","base":"PLEASE"}," of life. People paid an ",{"n":19,"answer":"admission","answers":["admission","admittance"],"explanation":"admission/admittance. Admission (or admittance) charge is the payment you need to make in order to be allowed to go inside something like a night club or any other paid place or event.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ADMIT"}," charge of one penny to enter a coffee shop, to enjoy the supposedly ",{"n":20,"answer":"beneficial","answers":["beneficial"],"explanation":"beneficial. Answer 'benefitting' is wrong as it would imply that effects themselves benefit from something.","skill":"Part 3","base":"BENEFIT"}," effects of the drink. Doctors at the time believed it could cure several diseases, and many drinkers reported that coffee made them more ",{"n":21,"answer":"energetic","answers":["energetic","energized","energised"],"explanation":"energetic/energized/energised. Even though 'energetic' and 'energised' have different meanings, both can be used here. 'Energetic' means having energy as a part of character; this could mean that coffee would have a long-term effect on the way a person is. 'Energised' simply means higher than usual levels of energy.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ENERGY"}," and improved their mood. ",{"n":22,"answer":"increasingly","answers":["increasingly"],"explanation":"increasingly. The point of the sentence is that coffee shops were more and more seen as a place of meeting, not just somewhere to have a cup of coffee.","skill":"Part 3","base":"INCREASE"},", a visit to a coffee shop had a serious purpose too, as people started to meet there to discuss politics and new ideas. Good behaviour was essential and if you were ",{"n":23,"answer":"impolite","answers":["impolite"],"explanation":"impolite. The negative meaning of the adjective is clearly suggested by context: '... you could be thrown out of a shop'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"POLITE"},", you could be thrown out of a shop. However, coffee shops in Europe declined in popularity in the late eighteenth century due to the greater ",{"n":24,"answer":"consumption","answers":["consumption"],"explanation":"consumption. A noun is needed here. Note that using -ing suffix for the answer (making either gerunds or participles/adjectives) is discouraged in FCE Use of English Part 3 and should not be used if there is a better alternative.","skill":"Part 3","base":"CONSUME"}," of tea, a drink that was easier to make than coffee."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Nobody explained why the flight had been delayed.","keyword":"REASON","secondBefore":"Nobody gave ","secondAfter":" to the flight.","answers":["a reason for the delay"],"display":"a reason for the delay","explanation":"a reason for the delay. 'A reason for something'. The choice of preposition here is dictated by the noun 'reason'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"Last year's skiing holiday was more exciting than this year's holiday by the sea.","keyword":"AS","secondBefore":"This year's holiday by the sea ","secondAfter":" last year's skiing holiday.","answers":["is not as exciting as"],"display":"is not as exciting as","explanation":"is not as exciting as. 'As ... as ...' structure is used to make a comparison here.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"Maria doesn't think we should visit the museum because it's not very interesting.","keyword":"POINT","secondBefore":"Maria says ","secondAfter":" the museum because it's not very interesting.","answers":["there is no point visiting"],"display":"there is no point visiting","explanation":"there is no point visiting. A tricky sentence that needs two words to be omitted (in brackets) in order to fit the required limit of five words: Maria says (that) there is no point (in) visiting the museum.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"All of us are excited about our trip to China next month.","keyword":"LOOKING","secondBefore":"Everyone ","secondAfter":" our trip to China next month.","answers":["is looking forward to"],"display":"is looking forward to","explanation":"is looking forward to. A thing to note here is that 'everyone' is grammatically singular, therefore we use 'is' and not 'are'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"When I was in Sweden, I managed to learn some Swedish.","keyword":"PICK","secondBefore":"When I was in Sweden, I was ","secondAfter":" some Swedish.","answers":["able to pick up"],"display":"able to pick up","explanation":"able to pick up. 'To pick something up' in relation to a skill is to learn some of it.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"The architecture here makes me think of the buildings in Amsterdam.","keyword":"REMINDS","secondBefore":"The architecture here ","secondAfter":" of the buildings in Amsterdam.","answers":["reminds me"],"display":"reminds me","explanation":"reminds me. Only two words used here, fitting the minimal possible transformation. This is a rare, but possible situation.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-27","examNumber":27,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 17","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"27 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 17","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Coming second: pleasure or pain?","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["Every ambitious athlete hopes to 0 _____ their dream of winning a gold medal at the Olympics. However, not everyone can win, and often talented athletes must accept second place. A team of psychologists recently ",{"n":1,"options":["controlled","conducted","directed","guided"],"answer":"conducted","answers":["conducted"],"explanation":"B - conducted. 'To conduct research' is the only fitting collocation in the given context. Of course, you could also control research (Answer A), but the phrase has a different meaning (to oversee or direct it).","skill":"Part 1"}," some research on the emotional responses of those finishing second. For certain individuals, a silver medal may ",{"n":2,"options":["exceed","overtake","pass","overcome"],"answer":"exceed","answers":["exceed"],"explanation":"A - exceed. This time, it is the only collocation that works with the word 'expectations'.","skill":"Part 1"}," their expectations and so naturally they will be delighted. They may also enjoy surprising experts and journalists who believed they had absolutely no ",{"n":3,"options":["outlook","view","estimate","prospect"],"answer":"prospect","answers":["prospect"],"explanation":"D - prospect. A prospect is chance or likelihood of something happening. 'Estimate' is more commonly used with numbers. 'Outlook' is a prediction, but is commonly used in a phrase like 'outlook for/of something'.","skill":"Part 1"}," of achieving anything. In ",{"n":4,"options":["opposition","contrast","distinction","contradiction"],"answer":"contrast","answers":["contrast"],"explanation":"B - contrast. An introductory phrase that makes a counter-point is needed here - 'In contrast'","skill":"Part 1"}," , the athlete who everyone assumed would win with ease, but then suffers a ",{"n":5,"options":["thin","tight","narrow","slight"],"answer":"narrow","answers":["narrow"],"explanation":"C - narrow. A narrow defeat is a defeat that was almost a victory, like in a situation where the losing athlete is only 0.01 seconds slower than the victor. Similarly, a narrow victory is one with minimal possible advantage over the second place (the runner-up position).","skill":"Part 1"}," defeat, may not celebrate their silver medal. This reaction differs sharply from the athlete who comes second but finished a long way behind the winner. There is a ",{"n":6,"options":["habit","trend","custom","tendency"],"answer":"trend","answers":["trend"],"explanation":"B - trend. In the context of a scientific analysis, a trend is something like a general finding that is repeated through multiple trials. In other words, it is something that happens many times and therefore cannot be seen as random.","skill":"Part 1"}," in the research that shows such a person will feel significantly happier. To a certain ",{"n":7,"options":["extent","amount","range","level"],"answer":"extent","answers":["extent"],"explanation":"A - extent. 'To some extent, to some degree' are phrases that mean that something is partially true, but not completely so..","skill":"Part 1"},", these findings are not surprising. Silver medallists who were close to victory will almost certainly ",{"n":8,"options":["review","wonder","consider","reflect"],"answer":"reflect","answers":["reflect"],"explanation":"D - reflect. To reflect on something means to think about something hard, to try and analyse it. If we disregard the preposition 'on', the other verbs would collocate differently: 'wonder about', 'consider something', 'review something'. However, contextually they do not work here.","skill":"Part 1"}," on what might have happened if they had trained harder, or done things differently."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · The importance of friendship","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["It is undeniable that friendship is important for just 0 _____ everyone. Individuals with several close friends are usually happier than those ",{"n":9,"answer":"without","answers":["without"],"explanation":"without. 'With' and 'without' here are used for added contrast. Omission of the word in the second part makes it harder to guess: '... than those without (them)'.","skill":"Part 2"},". Good friends often know things that family members may not be aware of, even ",{"n":10,"answer":"if","answers":["if","after"],"explanation":"if/after. 'Even if' is used to reinforce the point made in the sentence. It is also more natural of the two possible answers. 'After' is also acceptable.","skill":"Part 2"}," they have lived together for years. This is probably ",{"n":11,"answer":"because","answers":["because"],"explanation":"because. 'Because' helps make a connection with the previous sentence and explain the points made there.","skill":"Part 2"}," when in the company of a good friend we share our secrets and dreams. Friends turn to one ",{"n":12,"answer":"another","answers":["another"],"explanation":"another. 'One another' is a common combination that you should be familiar with at B2.","skill":"Part 2"}," for suggestions on how to solve their problems. There is a tendency ",{"n":13,"answer":"among","answers":["among"],"explanation":"among. 'Among' here expresses the idea 'in a particular group of people'.","skill":"Part 2"}," close friends to be very honest, sometimes saying things the other person may not want to hear!  Good friends stick together, and the best relationships may last a lifetime. It is ",{"n":14,"answer":"no","answers":["no"],"explanation":"no. Contextually, we see that the negative form is used for emphasis - to make the sentence more expressive, that is.","skill":"Part 2"}," surprise that most friends have similar personalities, which reduces the risk of conflict. However, people don't always have an accurate picture of ",{"n":15,"answer":"who","answers":["who"],"explanation":"who. In order to make the right choice here, we have to keep on reading. In this case, only the very last sentence in the paragraph provides sufficient context to answer correctly.","skill":"Part 2"}," their true friends are. Research shows that in a surprising number of cases a person someone considers a good friend doesn't feel the ",{"n":16,"answer":"same","answers":["same"],"explanation":"same. To feel the same about somebody means to have similar feelings towards them, either positive or negative ones. Here they talk about the idea of friendship that might not always be mutual.","skill":"Part 2"}," about them."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Lake Titicaca","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["Lake Titicaca is the largest lake in South America with the 0 _____ of Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela, which unlike Titicaca is connected directly to the sea. It is 190 kilometres long and reaches a maximum ",{"n":17,"answer":"depth","answers":["depth"],"explanation":"depth. Be careful with the spelling of this noun. Note that there is no such word as 'deepness' - a common wrong answer in this task.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DEEP"}," of 284 metres. Approximately 60 per cent of the lake lies in Peru and the rest in Bolivia. The lake is famous for a variety of ",{"n":18,"answer":"unusual","answers":["unusual"],"explanation":"unusual. Naturally, it is impossible to be famous for something usual, so we need a prefix with the opposite meaning.","skill":"Part 3","base":"USUAL"}," wildlife, including a giant frog that can weigh up to three kilogrammes. Some animals in the lake are ",{"n":19,"answer":"endangered","answers":["endangered"],"explanation":"endangered. If an animal is endangered, it means it is at risk of extinction (disappearing) because there are too few of them left. Note that 'dangerous' is wrong - the added context of a national reserve in the second part of the sentence hints at the fact the animal is in danger of disappearing.","skill":"Part 3","base":"DANGER"}," species, which led to the ",{"n":20,"answer":"establishment","answers":["establishment"],"explanation":"establishment. Establishment here means 'creation'. Again, 'establishing' will most likely not be considered the right answer, so avoid using gerund forms in FCE Use of English Part 3.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ESTABLISH"}," of Titicaca National Reserve in 1978. The protection of the wildlife is guaranteed, and so is the beautiful scenery that makes a visit to the lake so ",{"n":21,"answer":"unforgettable","answers":["unforgettable"],"explanation":"unforgettable. It is clear that we need an adjective with the meaning of 'memorable' here. Just make sure to spell this word correctly!","skill":"Part 3","base":"FORGET"},". There are numerous islands on the lake, although not all are ",{"n":22,"answer":"accessible","answers":["accessible"],"explanation":"accessible. Available, open, free.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ACCESS"}," to tourists. The ",{"n":23,"answer":"inhabitants","answers":["inhabitants"],"explanation":"inhabitants. Two things to note here - first of all, the noun has to be plural because it is then referred to as 'people'. Secondly, mind your spelling here, as the right word spelled the wrong way will not give you points.","skill":"Part 3","base":"INHABIT"}," of some of the islands are known as the Uros people, who still maintain their traditional way of life, but at the same time welcome visitors. ",{"n":24,"answer":"surprisingly","answers":["surprisingly"],"explanation":"surprisingly. The adverb has to express the idea of being surprised, because despite the beauty this place is known for, tourists don't go there too often.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SURPRISE"},", this beautiful lake is often ignored by tourists."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Some schools were closed for a couple of days because of the heavy snow.","keyword":"LED","secondBefore":"The heavy snow ","secondAfter":" for a couple of days.","answers":["led schools to getting closed","led schools to being closed"],"display":"led schools to getting closed","explanation":"led schools to getting/being closed. The structure 'lead to' is followed by a noun or gerund (-ing form), hence 'getting' or 'being'. Moreover, make sure not to exceed the word limit. To do that, we have to omit 'some': led (some) schools to getting/being closed .","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"Steve doesn't take much notice of the weather forecast.","keyword":"ATTENTION","secondBefore":"Steve doesn't ","secondAfter":" the weather forecast","answers":["doesn't pay much attention"],"display":"doesn't pay much attention","explanation":"doesn't pay much attention. Note that 'doesn't' is counted as two words - you can use both the shortened and the full form. Answering 'doesn't pay any attention' might only give you 1 of the 2 points - make sure to keep the changed sentence as close to the original one as you can.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"John wishes he had taken an umbrella to work this morning.","keyword":"TAKING","secondBefore":"John ","secondAfter":" an umbrella to work this morning","answers":["regrets not taking"],"display":"regrets not taking","explanation":"regrets not taking. The reason we have to use 'regret' is because this is the only fitting verb that would take the - ing form of 'take' in the given limited number of words.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"Donna said that apart from Friday, every day last week had been really hot.","keyword":"EXCEPTION","secondBefore":"Donna said that ","secondAfter":" Friday, every day last week had been really hot.","answers":["with the exception of"],"display":"with the exception of","explanation":"with the exception of. The definite article is required here as the integral part of the 'of' phrase.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"This is the worst weather we've ever had in July.","keyword":"AS","secondBefore":"We've ","secondAfter":" as this in July","answers":["never had weather as bad"],"display":"never had weather as bad","explanation":"never had weather as bad. 'As ... as ...' comparative structure is pretty common for FCE Use of English 4, so be ready to see it. Note that Present Perfect is used here.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"I'm sure it was unbearably hot where you were staying.","keyword":"MUST","secondBefore":"The ","secondAfter":" unbearable where you were staying.","answers":["heat must have been"],"display":"heat must have been","explanation":"heat must have been. A very tricky transformation; it requires the word 'heat'. Note that 'weather' doesn't fit here: if you say that the weather was unbearable, it could mean anything - hot or cold, raining or even snowing.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-28","examNumber":28,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 18","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"28 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 18","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · What our homes say about us","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["A home is more than a building. It's a 0 _____ which both influences our identity and represents it. As children, our environment makes us who we are, or at ",{"n":1,"options":["little","least","minimum","less"],"answer":"least","answers":["least"],"explanation":"B - least. 'At least' is the only real collocation here that works with 'at'. The other options could be 'a little' or 'more or less', however, we are always limited by the immediate context, in this case the preposition 'at'.","skill":"Part 1"}," contributes to it. For example, if we grow up in a crowded home, the thought of moving to a quiet home alone might never ",{"n":2,"options":["appear","inspire","attract","interest"],"answer":"appear","answers":["appear"],"explanation":"A - appear. 'Attract', 'inspire' and 'interest' all require no preposition to be used with them.","skill":"Part 1"}," to us. Instead, we may prefer to live in a house full of people for the ",{"n":3,"options":["final","total","last","rest"],"answer":"rest","answers":["rest"],"explanation":"D - rest. 'For the rest of something' means till the end of it. 'Last days' or 'final days' could also work in a slightly different context.","skill":"Part 1"}," of our lives. As adults, every object that ",{"n":4,"options":["owns","possesses","occupies","belongs"],"answer":"belongs","answers":["belongs"],"explanation":"D - belongs. If something belongs to you, it means that you are the owner of it. 'Belongs' is correct because we use the collocation belong to somebody. The other verbs would require a direct object (e.g. 'owns something'), which does not fit the structure of the sentence.","skill":"Part 1"}," to us tells a story about who we are and where we came from. In ",{"n":5,"options":["furthermore","addition","together","extra"],"answer":"addition","answers":["addition"],"explanation":"B - addition. 'In addition' is an introductory phrase we use to add more arguments to a previously mentioned point. 'Furthermore' serves a similar function, but does not require the preposition 'in'.","skill":"Part 1"},", how we present those objects in our homes says something about how we want others to see us. If we identify ourselves with being creative, for example, we might put certain art work on ",{"n":6,"options":["exhibition","gallery","display","public"],"answer":"display","answers":["display"],"explanation":"C - display. If you put something on display, you place it so as to make it easily noticeable in order for others to see it. Other options do not work with the preposition 'on'.","skill":"Part 1"}," for others to see. Or, if we believe that it's more important for a home to be relaxing than fashionable, our home might ",{"n":7,"options":["consist","include","contain","involve"],"answer":"consist","answers":["consist"],"explanation":"A - consist. While other verbs have a very similar or even exactly the same meaning, none of them require 'of' to work - they are used without any preposition in this context.","skill":"Part 1"}," of warm colours and comfortable furniture, in ",{"n":8,"options":["opposite","contrast","variety","difference"],"answer":"contrast","answers":["contrast"],"explanation":"B - contrast. If something is used in contrast to (or with) something else, they are compared to highlight their differences.","skill":"Part 1"}," to stylish, modern but perhaps more uncomfortable pieces."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · A cure for insomnia?","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["I've just read an article about blue light and I think I've discovered 0 _____ most likely reason for my recent restless nights! You see, blue light, emitted by sunshine, some types ",{"n":9,"answer":"of","answers":["of"],"explanation":"of. 'Types' is a plural noun here, not a verb, so we use the preposition 'of' to specify the types further in the sentence.","skill":"Part 2"}," lighting and electronic device screens, has both benefits and dangers for us. It helps improve mood, memory and concentration, but if we ",{"n":10,"answer":"spend","answers":["spend"],"explanation":"spend. To spend time is to use it for something. Be careful not to answer 'waste' as it introduces a meaning that doing something is a bad way to spend time, which is not mentioned or implied in the sentence.","skill":"Part 2"}," a lot of time staring at screens, it can actually cause damage to our eyes. Interestingly, the article also points ",{"n":11,"answer":"out","answers":["out"],"explanation":"out. 'To point out' is a phrasal verb that means to indicate something, to draw somebody's attention to something worth noting or looking at.","skill":"Part 2"}," that blue light controls our body clocks and using electronic devices too much immediately ",{"n":12,"answer":"before","answers":["before"],"explanation":"before. Keep in mind that in English, 'go to bed' means to go and get into bed in order to sleep. The idea here is that using devices immediately before going to bed can disrupt sleep cycles. The meaning is not literal, in other words it does not mean walking to bed and lying down. 'After' is a wrong answer as it would make no sense.","skill":"Part 2"}," going to bed can disrupt our sleep cycles. My nights have ",{"n":13,"answer":"been","answers":["been"],"explanation":"been. This form of the verb 'to be' is needed for the Present Perfect Continuous tense used in this sentence.","skill":"Part 2"}," getting worse recently and I'm sure it's because I ",{"n":14,"answer":"have","answers":["have"],"explanation":"have. This time we need 'have' to complete the Present Perfect Simple tense.","skill":"Part 2"}," just started reading on my tablet when I'm in bed. The experts' advice ",{"n":15,"answer":"is","answers":["is"],"explanation":"is. One can be tempted to give 'tells' as the answer - don't, as 'advice' is an uncountable noun and does not take 'tell' as a verb. We say 'advice is to…' or 'advice about…', but not 'advice tells…'.","skill":"Part 2"}," to avoid using bright screens for about two to  three hours before sleep, but I don't think I can restrict myself quite that much. Maybe, I'll switch ",{"n":16,"answer":"to","answers":["to"],"explanation":"to. 'To switch to something' means to make a change, to go from one option to a different one.","skill":"Part 2"}," reading a printed book and give social media a miss after nine o'clock!"]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Getting the look","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["It has been a long time since Coco Chanel first made it 0 _____ to have a tan. Before 1923 suntanned skin was considered both ",{"n":17,"answer":"unhealthy","answers":["unhealthy"],"explanation":"unhealthy. The only hint at the negative meaning (and the negative prefix un-) is the context of the second part of the sentence, how suntan was seen as something undesirable.","skill":"Part 3","base":"HEALTH"}," and a sign of poverty. Then Coco Chanel returned from a ",{"n":18,"answer":"sailing","answers":["sailing"],"explanation":"sailing. It is clear that we are looking for an adjective to modify the noun 'holiday' that comes after the gap. The only other possible thing that might come to mind is 'sailor', which of course makes little sense, especially keeping in mind Coco's glamorous background. A sailing holiday is a holiday spent in the sea on a yacht or other similarly large sea vessel.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SAIL"}," holiday with a tan and suddenly everyone else wanted one. Doctors' ",{"n":19,"answer":"advice","answers":["advice"],"explanation":"advice. All we do here is change the verb 'to advise' into the noun, 'advice'. Word transformation is done by changing just one letter here. Additionally, do not pluralise, as there is no plural form that says 'advices'. Instead, it is 'pieces of advice'. Remember that in FCE Use of English Part 3 you are always expected to make a change to the original word. The answer can never be the same word.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ADVISE"}," changed too. Sunshine was a source of Vitamin D. Unfortunately having beach holidays was ",{"n":20,"answer":"unaffordable","answers":["unaffordable"],"explanation":"unaffordable. Another case of context hinting at a negative form of the adjective.","skill":"Part 3","base":"AFFORDABLE"}," for many people in northern countries. In the 1940s, women who were ",{"n":21,"answer":"impatient","answers":["impatient"],"explanation":"impatient. The idea here is that some women were in a hurry to get suntan - they were impatient. The negative prefix 'un-' does not work for 'patient' (there is no 'umpatient' as a word in English), be careful not to make this common mistake.","skill":"Part 3","base":"PATIENT"}," to get a tanned look started to use a mixture made from tea bags and gravy to stain their legs brown. This was obviously impractical for a ",{"n":22,"answer":"variety","answers":["variety"],"explanation":"variety. A noun that has the vowel in the middle transformed, so be careful with your spelling. A misspelled word does not give you any points, even if you get the idea of the word itself correctly.","skill":"Part 3","base":"VARY"}," of reasons! Better solutions came after an accidental discovery in the 1950s when a ",{"n":23,"answer":"scientist","answers":["scientist"],"explanation":"scientist. The indefinite article before the gap hints at the singular form of the profession. Be sure to mind your spelling with this one.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SCIENCE"}," found that a safe chemical, DHA, could be used in a lotion to fake a tan. Medical research also showed the ",{"n":24,"answer":"irreversible","answers":["irreversible"],"explanation":"irreversible. The negative form, that is rather tricky to spell right. If something is irreversible, it means that it cannot be returned to the previous state.","skill":"Part 3","base":"REVERSIBLE"}," damage that sun can do to our skin and today fake tan is a much safer and more convenient alternative to sunbathing."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Julio thought people lived in the TV so he regularly talked to them.","keyword":"WOULD","secondBefore":"Julio ","secondAfter":" people on TV because he thought they lived in there.","answers":["would (regularly) talk to (the)"],"display":"would (regularly) talk to (the)","explanation":"would (regularly) talk to (the). It is important to understand why we need 'would' here as after that the words just fall into place. Here it has a similar meaning to 'used to' - that is, something that happened often in the past, but no longer does.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"Richard hasn't believed in monsters for years.","keyword":"LONG","secondBefore":"It's been ","secondAfter":" Richard believed in monsters.","answers":["a long time since"],"display":"a long time since","explanation":"a long time since. If it has been a long time since something, it means it hasn't happened for a while.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"That day was the first time that William realised his mistake.","keyword":"NEVER","secondBefore":"William ","secondAfter":" his mistake before that day.","answers":["had never realised","'d never realised"],"display":"had never realised","explanation":"had/'d never realised. We have to use Past Perfect here to make it clear which event happened before the other. We use Past Perfect for the event that happened before the one we use in Past Simple. Past Perfect happens first, Past Simple takes place later.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"Lucia thought that her brother's toys came alive at night.","keyword":"BELONGED","secondBefore":"Lucia believed that the toys ","secondAfter":" her brother came alive at night.","answers":["which belonged to","that belonged to"],"display":"which belonged to","explanation":"which/that belonged to. We use the structure to outline the ownership of the toys here.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"Francesca started telling stories before her brother was born.","keyword":"SINCE","secondBefore":"Francesca ","secondAfter":" before her brother was born.","answers":["has been telling stories since"],"display":"has been telling stories since","explanation":"has been telling stories since. Use Present Perfect Continuous here, as we are bound by the keyword 'since'.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"Luc was on a walk in the park when he saw something strange.","keyword":"THROUGH","secondBefore":"Luc saw something strange while he ","secondAfter":" the park.","answers":["was walking through"],"display":"was walking through","explanation":"was walking through. We use a set of tenses to show that some shorter action took place during the longer action. He saw something (the short action) while he was having a walk (the longer action).","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-29","examNumber":29,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 19","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"29 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 19","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Tips for making your own short film","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["These days it's easy to make a film. You don't need to 0 _____ expensive equipment, you can simply use your mobile phone. Start by ",{"n":1,"options":["starting","thinking","going","coming"],"answer":"coming","answers":["coming"],"explanation":"D - coming. 'To come up with something' means to think of something new, to invent something, to have a new idea. Another phrase from the list and a similar meaning is 'to think of something'.","skill":"Part 1"},"up with a good idea. You can ",{"n":2,"options":["check","figure","look","make"],"answer":"check","answers":["check"],"explanation":"A - check. If you check something out, you have a look at it to see if you like it or if it is any good. 'To figure out' means to solve some problem or to understand something. 'To make out' usually means to manage to see something that is not clear, e.g. to make out something in the distance.","skill":"Part 1"}," out lists of ideas online to give you inspiration. A good script can make all the difference so before filming, your script should become the ",{"n":3,"options":["attention","focus","target","attraction"],"answer":"focus","answers":["focus"],"explanation":"B - focus. Something that is the focus of your time is what you dedicate all of your attention to because it is the most important thing.","skill":"Part 1"}," of your time. Keep it simple and ",{"n":4,"options":["work","take","bring","try"],"answer":"work","answers":["work"],"explanation":"A - work. To work on something is to put your effort into it. You can work on many things, such as a book, a diploma paper, a car, renovating your flat, and so on.","skill":"Part 1"}," on developing one character well in the short time you have. Select appropriate venues for filming. Avoid places where you'll be ",{"n":5,"options":["contained","grouped","closed","surrounded"],"answer":"surrounded","answers":["surrounded"],"explanation":"D - surrounded. Probably the easiest choice in this exercise, if you are surrounded by somebody or something, then there is too much of it next to you.","skill":"Part 1"}," by people who turn and stare at the camera. Make sure there's nothing unusual in the ",{"n":6,"options":["window","conditions","background","context"],"answer":"background","answers":["background"],"explanation":"C - background. Background here refers to what is happening behind the main focus of the camera. If there is something happening there, it might take over the viewers' attention.","skill":"Part 1"}," to distract from what the main actor's doing. And make sure the light is right. You want to give the ",{"n":7,"options":["image","attitude","impression","theory"],"answer":"impression","answers":["impression"],"explanation":"C - impression. 'The impression of depth' is a visual effect that makes the image look more three-dimensional, more 'real'. The author is talking about how light and shadows should work together to achieve such an effect.","skill":"Part 1"}," of depth, not have strange shadows covering the actor's face. Finally, when you've finished filming and edited your work, show it to friends and get some feedback. No film-maker can ",{"n":8,"options":["accomplish","win","overcome","succeed"],"answer":"succeed","answers":["succeed"],"explanation":"D - succeed. To achieve success. Note that 'accomplish' cannot be used here as it requires a direct object without preposition: 'to accomplish something', but 'to succeed IN something'.","skill":"Part 1"}," in the film industry without that."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · A festival of colour","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["It's early March and people in India are preparing 0 _____ Holi, a spring festival which signals the end of winter. It's an event which has been ",{"n":9,"answer":"taking","answers":["taking"],"explanation":"taking. To take place means to happen, to occur. Note the continuous form of the verb - as dictated by the context: \"It's an event which has been...\".","skill":"Part 2"}," place for centuries, and one which ",{"n":10,"answer":"is","answers":["is"],"explanation":"is. Another case of Present Continuous, this time we need to use the auxiliary 'to be' in the right form.","skill":"Part 2"}," attracting more and more tourists from around the world.  On day one of the festival, towns and cities become awash with colour. People light bonfires and play music, sing and dance. They place coloured powder ",{"n":11,"answer":"on","answers":["on"],"explanation":"on. When we talk about using a surface, whether it is a table, the floor, or somebody's face - we normally use 'on'. Do not use 'onto' as it indicates movement towards something, e.g. \"The players stepped onto the court for the final match\".","skill":"Part 2"}," each other's faces and throw some of that same powder in the air. Coloured water adds ",{"n":12,"answer":"to","answers":["to"],"explanation":"to. \"To add to something\" means to make something more complete, to complement it. If something adds to the fun, it means that it makes it more enjoyable.","skill":"Part 2"}," the fun too. Very quickly, people are covered in a variety of colours. The point of this is to ",{"n":13,"answer":"give","answers":["give"],"explanation":"give. 'Create' is not good enough here, as we usually say \"to create AN impression\". The difference is minor in meaning, but the usage of article plays a crucial role here.","skill":"Part 2"}," the impression that they ",{"n":14,"answer":"are","answers":["are"],"explanation":"are. We need a verb in this sentence, so the auxiliary 'to be' in the plural form is required in the gap.","skill":"Part 2"}," all equal. Day two is a much quieter affair. People ",{"n":15,"answer":"have","answers":["have"],"explanation":"have. If you have a tendency to do something, it means that you are likely to do it. The meaning is similar to the verb 'tend to', from which the noun is derived.","skill":"Part 2"}," a tendency to spend it with family, after they've cleaned up of course. If you have ",{"n":16,"answer":"ever","answers":["ever"],"explanation":"ever. The test-taker might be tempted to answer 'already', but the second part of the sentence expresses the idea of experience better. It is more common to see a structure with 'ever' to talk about something you have experienced in the past.","skill":"Part 2"}," attended Holi, you'll know that it's not a place to wear your best clothes. It's also a good idea to place oil on your skin so that it doesn't absorb the powder - otherwise it can be impossible to remove."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · An inspirational city","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["The 0 _____ city of Jaipur in India is known by two names; 'The Paris of India' and 'The Pink City'. The first relates to its superb architecture and ",{"n":17,"answer":"romantic","answers":["romantic"],"explanation":"romantic. As we are looking for an adjective to modify the word 'atmosphere' that comes right after the gap, we find ourselves stuck choosing between 'romantic' and 'romantical'. The fact is, 'romantical' is used way less often and is pretty much deprecated nowadays.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ROMANCE"}," atmosphere and the second to the fact that the majority of buildings are painted pink! One of Jaipur's prominent ",{"n":18,"answer":"attractions","answers":["attractions"],"explanation":"attractions. It is easy to understand that we need a noun here. However, the more tricky part is to realise that we are looking for a plural form - the hint is the structure 'one of...', which introduces plurality.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ATTRACT"},", the Hawa Mahal, is a good example of both names. The stunning structure, built in the shape of a crown, is ",{"n":19,"answer":"impressive","answers":["impressive"],"explanation":"impressive. The only confusion here can be caused by the commas surrounding the clause. Be careful to get the spelling of the word right.","skill":"Part 3","base":"IMPRESS"},", and its intense colour is typical of the city. Just why is Jaipur mainly pink? One ",{"n":20,"answer":"explanation","answers":["explanation"],"explanation":"explanation. The word 'one' is a clear giveaway that we need to be picking a singular form this time.","skill":"Part 3","base":"EXPLAIN"}," is that when Prince Albert visited in 1876, the ruler had the whole city painted in the symbolic Indian colour of ",{"n":21,"answer":"hospitality","answers":["hospitality"],"explanation":"hospitality. Hospitality is the quality of being open and welcoming to guests.","skill":"Part 3","base":"HOSPITABLE"},". Another theory is that most of the buildings had been built of pink sandstone and the ruler was ",{"n":22,"answer":"simply","answers":["simply"],"explanation":"simply. An adverb that suggests a more likely explanation of the curious colour chosen for local architecture.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SIMPLE"}," making the city look more uniform. Whatever the reason, Jaipur's beauty is known ",{"n":23,"answer":"internationally","answers":["internationally"],"explanation":"internationally. It is only by reading the next sentence that we get the idea for the word in the gap. Since people from other countries are drawn to the city, it means that its fame is international.","skill":"Part 3","base":"NATIONAL"},". Tourists and artists are drawn from all over the world by the ",{"n":24,"answer":"originality","answers":["originality"],"explanation":"originality. Originality means that the thing in question is unique, rather than a copy or an imitation of something else.","skill":"Part 3","base":"ORIGINAL"}," of its colourful appearance."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"'Do you know when the next cloudless night is?' my brother asked.","keyword":"IF","secondBefore":"My brother asked me ","secondAfter":" the next cloudless night was.","answers":["if I knew when"],"display":"if I knew when","explanation":"if I knew when. 'If' is used to report so-called yes/no questions, otherwise known as closed questions. Keep in mind that when reporting somebody's speech, we normally take the verb one step back in tense, e.g. Present Simple becomes Past Simple in its reported form.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"'What time did Sofia leave this morning?' asked Mack.","keyword":"SET","secondBefore":"Mack wondered what time Sofia ","secondAfter":" that morning.","answers":["had set off","had set out"],"display":"had set off","explanation":"had set off/out. We are forced to rephrase 'leave' through a phrasal verb with some choice - both 'set off' and 'set out' work fine here.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"'Don't bother looking for the Northern Lights tonight' Marta said.","keyword":"TOLD","secondBefore":"Marta ","secondAfter":" looking for the Northern Lights tonight.","answers":["told me not to bother"],"display":"told me not to bother","explanation":"told me not to bother. If you are told not to bother doing something, they mean that there is no use trying to do it for one reason or another.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"'Please don't make a mistake when you book our flights' said Andy.","keyword":"MESS","secondBefore":"Andy asked Ben ","secondAfter":" when he booked their flights.","answers":["not to mess (it) up"],"display":"not to mess (it) up","explanation":"not to mess (it) up. Yet another case of substituting with a phrasal verb, to mess something up means to do something badly or to fail to do it altogether.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"'I've been doing some fascinating activities this year' said Natalia.","keyword":"PARTICIPATING","secondBefore":"Natalia told me that ","secondAfter":" some fascinating activities that year.","answers":["she had been participating in"],"display":"she had been participating in","explanation":"she had been participating in. We are changing Preset Perfect Continuous in the direct speech to Past Perfect Continuous in the reported sentence. Another change is using the dictated 'to participate in something' structure.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"'Did you see any memorable sunsets on your trip?' Philip asked.","keyword":"ACROSS","secondBefore":"Philip asked me ","secondAfter":" any memorable sunsets on my trip.","answers":["if I had come across"],"display":"if I had come across","explanation":"if I had come across. 'To come across something' means to find or see something, especially if you didn't mean to or it was not your original intention.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}},{"id":"exam-30","examNumber":30,"label":"ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 20","source":{"type":"EngExam","label":"30 · ENGEXAM · RUOE · Practice Test 20","detail":"Reading & Use of English practice test stored in the master Drive bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · The migration of the monarch butterflies","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","segments":["The migration of millions of North American monarch butterflies is one of the most stunning 0 _____ phenomena on Earth. In ",{"n":1,"options":["contrast","difference","variation","disagreement"],"answer":"contrast","answers":["contrast"],"explanation":"A - contrast. If something is in contrast with something else, then it is very different from it. 'In disagreement with' could be another legit collocation here, but as its form suggests, it does not agree with something, i.e. it does not work well with it.","skill":"Part 1"}," to other members of the same species elsewhere, the North American species migrates far in winter, taking a journey that ",{"n":2,"options":["involves","consists","includes","counts"],"answer":"consists","answers":["consists"],"explanation":"B - consists. Just like in the previous case, we have to choose a word that works well with the preposition that comes after the gap. All three other options would normally be used without any preposition.","skill":"Part 1"}," of 3000 miles. They ",{"n":3,"options":["start","depart","launch","leave"],"answer":"start","answers":["start"],"explanation":"A - start. To start off is a phrasal verb that means to begin a journey. 'Depart' needs no preposition, 'leave' could be used with 'for' here, e.g. 'He left for another country many years ago'.","skill":"Part 1"}," off in Canada and the US, arrive in central Mexico in November, and return in March. The sight of them together in fir trees in Mexico is an incredible ",{"n":4,"options":["stage","context","spot","scene"],"answer":"scene","answers":["scene"],"explanation":"D - scene. We are looking for a phrase here that expresses the idea of something we can see (witness). The word 'scene' works the best. In some languages, the word 'stage' might have a similar meaning, be careful not to get fooled by this similarity.","skill":"Part 1"}," to witness. However, none of the butterflies actually make the entire round trip. The females lay eggs in Mexico and it's the new generation that heads north. They travel to Texas where the process is repeated. The butterflies have a ",{"n":5,"options":["habit","tendency","trend","custom"],"answer":"tendency","answers":["tendency"],"explanation":"B - tendency. If you have a tendency to do something, it means you do it often; you are likely to do it. We say 'to have a habit of doing something', which is why we can't use it here. The word 'custom' is more appropriate for a cultural thing, and since we are talking about butterflies, it just doesn't work too well here.","skill":"Part 1"}," to stop twice on their way back to Canada, meaning that it takes four generations to complete the trip. Monarch butterflies are important because they pollinate plants. In ",{"n":6,"options":["addition","extension","comparison","conclusion"],"answer":"addition","answers":["addition"],"explanation":"A - addition. The paragraph focuses on how these butterflies benefit nature, and the introductory phrase 'in addition' helps us to name another benefit they have.","skill":"Part 1"},", they form a key part of the food chain. They also ",{"n":7,"options":["undergo","face","suffer","take"],"answer":"face","answers":["face"],"explanation":"B - face. If you face something, especially something negative, such as danger or threat, it means that you are likely to be affected by it. Other verbs here do not collocate with 'danger'.","skill":"Part 1"}," danger from disease and a loss of habitat. However, at ",{"n":8,"options":["best","minimum","least","part"],"answer":"least","answers":["least"],"explanation":"C - least. We use 'at least' here to introduce a positive aspect in an otherwise negative context of the paragraph.","skill":"Part 1"}," their forest in Mexico is protected."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · A sculptor's home","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","segments":["I'm currently sitting outside La Villa des Brilliants, once 0 _____ home of well-known sculptor Auguste Rodin. Rodin purchased this property on the outskirts of Paris in 1895. By 1900, he ",{"n":9,"answer":"had","answers":["had"],"explanation":"had. We use Past Perfect is used with 'by + past time' to describe an action completed before that time. Even if you, like many other students of this level, feel overwhelmed by Past Perfect tense, there is not much room for choice here.","skill":"Part 2"}," turned it into a home and a studio where he worked until his death in 1917. As well as having around 50 assistants, Rodin ",{"n":10,"answer":"would","answers":["would"],"explanation":"would. Use 'would' to show a repeated action in the past that no longer happens. In that role, it is very similar to the more common structure 'used to do something'.","skill":"Part 2"}," regularly invite friends, family and art lovers to visit his studio.  The house has been a museum ",{"n":11,"answer":"since","answers":["since"],"explanation":"since. Be careful not to answer 'after' - first, because we have a word 'since' that suggests the usage of Perfect Tense. Secondly, with 'after' the idea of it still being a museum gets lost.","skill":"Part 2"}," Rodin's death. Photographs were used to renovate the house in the latter part of the last century, so now we can see exactly what it was ",{"n":12,"answer":"like","answers":["like"],"explanation":"like. Here we talk about the resemblance of the current design of his house and that existed during the sculptor's lifetime.","skill":"Part 2"}," when Rodin lived there. What's special about coming here ",{"n":13,"answer":"is","answers":["is"],"explanation":"is. We use the auxiliary 'to be' to introduce a so-called 'cleft sentence'. It is used to shift the focus of the sentence as well as to make it more impactful.","skill":"Part 2"}," that we can see his most famous works of art, as well as ",{"n":14,"answer":"get","answers":["get"],"explanation":"get. To get a sense of something means to understand what something is like. While 'have' technically works in this context, it does not indicate the idea of change, i.e. starting to understand what kind of daily life the sculptor had.","skill":"Part 2"}," a sense of his daily life. Rodin is buried in the grounds of the house, under a statue of The Thinker, one of his most well-loved pieces. Needless to ",{"n":15,"answer":"say","answers":["say"],"explanation":"say. An introductory phrase that is usually used with statements that might seem obvious or unnecessary.","skill":"Part 2"},", this is a place where artists such as myself can pay respects to the man we look ",{"n":16,"answer":"up","answers":["up"],"explanation":"up. If you look up to somebody, it means you have profound respect for them, you see them as an idol.","skill":"Part 2"}," to."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Is regifting ever acceptable?","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits the gap.","segments":["How would you feel if you spent time buying someone a gift and then found out that they went and gave it to someone else? Would you be happy that it had found a 0 _____ who wanted it, or would you think it was incredibly ",{"n":17,"answer":"impolite","answers":["impolite"],"explanation":"impolite. In contrast with the first part of the sentence, we are looking for a negative adjective here. Then the only thing remaining is to find the right negative prefix.","skill":"Part 3","base":"POLITE"},"? The act of regifting unwanted gifts is gaining in ",{"n":18,"answer":"popularity","answers":["popularity"],"explanation":"popularity. To gain in popularity means to become more widespread, to turn into a more popular option.","skill":"Part 3","base":"POPULAR"},". It keeps our homes tidy, saves waste and makes gift-giving more ",{"n":19,"answer":"affordable","answers":["affordable"],"explanation":"affordable. Do not be tricked into using the word 'affording' as there is no such adjective.","skill":"Part 3","base":"AFFORD"},". However, there are unwritten rules we should follow when regifting items that we're ",{"n":20,"answer":"dissatisfied","answers":["dissatisfied"],"explanation":"dissatisfied. The context sends a clear signal that a negative word is needed. Note that 'dissatisfied' is more commonly seen in phrases like 'dissatisfied with something', whereas there is also 'unsatisfied' that is more appropriate as a descriptive adjective, e.g. 'unsatisfied people'.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SATISFY"}," with. We should avoid passing anything on that was made especially for us, even if we find it ",{"n":21,"answer":"unappealing","answers":["unappealing"],"explanation":"unappealing. Yet another negative adjective, 'unappealing' means not attractive or interesting to somebody.","skill":"Part 3","base":"APPEAL"},". We should also take care not to forget the ",{"n":22,"answer":"identity","answers":["identity"],"explanation":"identity. 'Identity of the gift giver' is just a fancy way of saying 'the person you got the gift from'. Identity in relation to people means the who the person is, e.g. 'the identity of the criminal remains unknown'. This means that they do not know who the criminal is, such as his name or background.","skill":"Part 3","base":"IDENTIFY"}," of the gift giver and accidentally give the gift back to them. Giving anyone the opportunity to discover their gift was unwanted is ",{"n":23,"answer":"insensitive","answers":["insensitive"],"explanation":"insensitive. If something or somebody is insensitive, it means that they do not take other people's feelings into account.","skill":"Part 3","base":"SENSITIVE"}," and allowing it to happen is ",{"n":24,"answer":"irresponsible","answers":["irresponsible"],"explanation":"irresponsible. An irresponsible action is one that you choose not to be accountable for because you are not aware of the consequences or simply do not care.","skill":"Part 3","base":"RESPONSIBLE"},"."]},"4":{"title":"Part 4 · Key word transformations","subtitle":"Questions 25–30","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words, including the word given.","items":[{"n":25,"first":"Lily hasn't taken karate lessons since she was 16.","keyword":"WHEN","secondBefore":"Lily ","secondAfter":" she was 16.","answers":["stopped taking karate lessons when / gave up karate lessons when / last took karate lessons when"],"display":"stopped taking karate lessons when / gave up karate lessons when / last took karate lessons when","explanation":"stopped taking karate lessons when / gave up karate lessons when / last took karate lessons when. Note that the verb pattern is \"to stop doing something\", but \"to give up something\". This is a rare case when you have plenty of freedom with the phrasing. Remember that as long as you stay within the word limit and keep the meaning of the original sentence the same, you are free to phrase your answer however you want.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":26,"first":"The outcome of the meeting was unexpected.","keyword":"TURN","secondBefore":"The meeting didn't ","secondAfter":" we had expected.","answers":["turn out as / turn out how / turn out the way"],"display":"turn out as / turn out how / turn out the way","explanation":"turn out as / turn out how / turn out the way. To turn out means to have something as a result, e.g. \"the party turned out to be a great success\". Once again, you are given plenty of choice in phrasing your answer.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":27,"first":"The price of petrol went up again a moment ago.","keyword":"JUST","secondBefore":"The price of petrol ","secondAfter":" again.","answers":["has just gone up"],"display":"has just gone up","explanation":"has just gone up. The usage of Present Perfect is needed here to stress that the price increase happened at this very moment. The word 'just' usually implies the usage of a Perfect tense.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":28,"first":"I was on a skiing trip in the Alps when I met Tom.","keyword":"WHILE","secondBefore":"I met Tom ","secondAfter":" skiing in the Alps.","answers":["while I was"],"display":"while I was","explanation":"while I was. A combination of a continuous tense for a longer action (\"was skiing\") and a shorter action that took place during the long one (\"I met Tom\").","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":29,"first":"You must subscribe to use the music site.","keyword":"HAVE","secondBefore":"You have ","secondAfter":" use the music site.","answers":["to have a subscription to"],"display":"to have a subscription to","explanation":"to have a subscription to. You might get confused by the two cases of 'have' here. One is a modal, with a meaning \"need to\", the other one is used in the more traditional sense of possession. The meaning here is that you need to have a subscription.","skill":"Part 4"},{"n":30,"first":"I often chatted to an invisible friend when I was a child.","keyword":"USED","secondBefore":"I ","secondAfter":" an invisible friend when I was a child.","answers":["(often) used to chat to"],"display":"(often) used to chat to","explanation":"(often) used to chat to. We choose \"used to\" for repeated actions in the past that no longer take place.","skill":"Part 4"}]}}}];
